# THE ATLAS OF UNWRITTEN SKIES
# A complete original branching fantasy novella, authored for Vesper.
# Syntax of this editable source: @scene / !command / Mira: / Cael: / plain narration / ? "Choice" -> scene

@scene prologue
!set mercy = 0
!set truth = 0
!set wonder = 0
!set bond = 0
!set promise = "none"
!set witness = "none"
!set debt = "none"
!background observatory_evening
!music archive
!panel parchment
!show mira open at left
The last map in the world had a hole in it. Not a tear: torn paper left fibers, a visible raggedness where the knife had passed. The Atlas of Unwritten Skies was intact. Its vellum shone like a shallow pond at sunrise, its coastlines remained fine as hairs, its copper letters had not dimmed in three hundred years. But where the city of Bellwether should have stood, someone had rubbed away the fact that a city had ever existed.
The observatory had always insisted that the sky was legible. Every instrument had a purpose, every shelf a number, and every mistake a procedure for correction. That morning, before Cael arrived, Mira had discovered a different kind of mistake in the oldest catalog. The book contained an index for eight hundred and twelve entries, but between the headings for tides and translation there were seven empty pages, each numbered in a careful copper hand. The missing entries occupied space. They had not left their subjects behind.
She had held the book up to the window and seen impressions in the paper where someone had pressed a pen hard enough to mark the leaves beneath. On page three, a sentence was still partly visible: A HOUSE IS A KIND OF PROMISE MADE BY A STREET. On page four, in a different script: DO NOT LET THE MINISTERS CALL IT WEATHER. The next impression was the shape of a child's hand, traced around five fingers. She copied the marks into her own ledger, knowing that writing them might preserve their shapes but not what they meant.
Her first impulse had been to report the discovery to the Council. The Council kept records, employed archivists, maintained centuries of observations, and paid Mira's salary when it remembered to pay anyone. Yet she could name three researchers who had sent warnings to the Council and become the subjects of warnings themselves. They were not in prison. That would at least have left a place where letters might be sent. Their rooms had been reassigned, their names removed from the rosters, and their colleagues trained to say that the vacancies were administrative matters. The Council had developed a remarkably efficient vocabulary for turning human beings into scheduling problems.
Mira set the ledger beside the atlas. She imagined the city as its inhabitants must have last seen it: morning bread cooling on a sill, a window opened for the smell of rain, an argument paused because someone had to leave for work. A disappearance was easy to think about when it belonged to a map. She was learning to think about the breakfast that went uneaten and the person who would never return to finish the argument. That was why she had stopped sleeping. It was also why she could not stop counting.
Mira Vale had known Bellwether in the ordinary way people know places they hope to return to. She remembered wet pavement and a window full of pears, an argument about whether the sea was green or blue, and her father's hand holding hers so she would not cross when the tram bell sounded. Now there was a patch of cream-colored paper in her father's atlas. She could remember him. She could no longer remember the sound of his voice.
The omission grew by one narrow street whenever the observatory clock struck twelve. Mira had taken to avoiding clocks. It had not helped.
!sound page
Mira: There was a bakery here. A blue door and a woman who insisted that the best bread was always sold out. Her husband played the violin badly, and everyone loved him for it.
She touched the blank place with the bone tip of a measuring instrument. The ink beside her fingertip shivered. A dot of coastline slipped away, leaving the harbor suspended without water. She had begun counting the losses in a notebook, because the losses did not count themselves.
The observatory stood on an arch of stone above a country of clouds. Its libraries occupied twelve stories; its lenses, suspended in revolving brass, were larger than rooms. Every evening the clouds below reflected the colors of a sunrise that would not occur for another ten hours. Some called this an optical marvel. Mira called it an intolerable habit of the sky, which ought to keep a more straightforward schedule.
In her ledger she had written: Seven bridges. Two hospitals. An orchard. The names of four hundred and seventeen people. For the last three nights she had written the same sentence underneath the list: I must find out who is doing this before the list learns to include me.
!ambient show spark
A small ember of blue light escaped the great celestial sphere above her. It drifted between brass rings, hesitated over the ledger, and settled on the drawing of the vanished harbor. Mira reached for it. Instead of heat, she felt the cold pressure of an unfamiliar memory: a boy laughing in a stairwell, a lantern breaking in a fountain, and a name spoken too softly to hear.
The pressure faded. The blue spark remained, trapped beneath her fingertip like the reflection of a tiny star.
Mira: Who are you supposed to be?
!sound bell
The west door rang, which was alarming. No one came to the west door. For thirty years it had opened onto a staircase that was not attached to a building, and people who attempted the stairs usually found themselves on a rooftop they had never visited. The door rang again with the persistence of somebody who had been told to wait and had decided waiting was merely an inefficient way of knocking.
Mira closed the atlas. She did not extinguish the lamp. There are ways to make a room seem unoccupied and ways to make it seem dangerous. She preferred the second.
!jump courier

@scene courier
!background observatory_close
!music archive
!show cael steady at right
The man outside wore the weather of three separate altitudes. His navy cloak had been soaked by rain and dried by wind; its hem had acquired a white rim of frost. A leather satchel rested against his hip. His boots were old but carefully repaired, and a telescope hung from a strap beside a pocket watch without hands. He looked at Mira's expression, then at the iron hook in her hand.
Cael: I have a delivery, but I can come back when the room is less committed to self-defense.
Mira: The post office stopped delivering to the observatory eleven years ago.
Cael: The post office stopped existing eleven years ago. Not quite the same thing.
The man offered an envelope without stepping over the threshold. Across it, in a script Mira knew better than her own, was a sentence that made the entire building seem to tilt: TO THE DAUGHTER I WILL NO LONGER REMEMBER.
She stared at it so long that the courier slowly lowered his arm. He did not joke again.
Mira: Where did you find this?
Cael: In Bellwether. Under the south platform, where the tracks ended in the sea. I have been carrying it for eight days. Each morning I woke with fewer memories of the place I had collected it. By yesterday I was not sure which direction the platform faced. Today I cannot remember the station at all, only the letter. I thought I had gone mad until I saw the hole in the map from your balcony.
Mira: You can see it from there?
Cael: There are lights under the cloudline. Not stars; stars would have the good sense to remain where they were put. Your observatory has a blue wound in its roof and all the birds avoid it.
Mira had never asked a stranger to describe the observatory from outside. She had never considered that its emptiness might be visible. The idea made her feel suddenly as if she had been living with a wound without noticing the blood on her clothes.
He said his name was Cael Vey. He had once worked the sky routes that threaded islands into the upper air. His company had gone bankrupt when the northern winds changed. A week later the company had not existed. His colleagues vanished from the records, although their signatures remained on his old pay slips. He kept the slips folded in oilskin, not for their monetary value, but to have the signatures of people who had once addressed him by name.
Cael: I used to think maps were promises that the world would keep its appointments. Now I think the world has been refusing to attend for some time.
Mira: The world is not refusing. Someone is cancelling the appointments.
Cael considered her for a moment, as though deciding whether she was dangerous because she knew something or because she knew too little. Then he held out the envelope again.
Cael: In that case, I would prefer not to be late.
!sound page
Mira recognized the seal. An eight-pointed compass star impressed in dark blue wax: her father's private mark, used when he wanted to tell her something the Council would never allow him to publish. She had last seen it seventeen years ago, when he disappeared into the storm beyond the western observatory.
The paper trembled in her hands. A strange delicacy was required to handle a letter from the dead. She wanted to tear it open with her teeth. She also wanted to put it back in its envelope and live forever in the moment before knowing what it contained.
? "Read the letter now, however much it hurts." -> letter_open
? "Ask Cael everything about the delivery first." -> courier_questions
? "Protect the atlas; make sure the letter cannot be a trap." -> protective_choice

@scene letter_open
!set truth += 2
Mira broke the seal with her thumbnail. Her father had always written as if the page were a room in which he expected to be overheard. The letters sloped rightward, then returned to the left at the ends of lines, correcting themselves in miniature as though even his handwriting could not settle on a destination.
The letter said: My darling Mira, if this reached you, I have failed to make the erasure gentle. There is no graceful way to unmake a place. I did not begin the work, but I helped keep it moving. I told myself that a city can be spared a catastrophe by never allowing it to have existed. I was wrong about what it costs. I am writing to the daughter who will have to choose whether to forgive a father she cannot remember.
She read the paragraph twice. At the third reading she noticed a different letter, paler than the ink around it, appearing along the margin. It was her own handwriting, although she had not written it: DO NOT LET THEM OPEN THE EASTERN LENS.
Mira: This isn't an apology. It's an instruction.
Cael: Can it be both?
Mira did not answer. She folded the letter along its old crease and put it beside the atlas, where two histories of her father could disagree in plain sight.
!jump atlas_wakes

@scene courier_questions
!set bond += 1
!set wonder += 1
Mira left the letter sealed and made tea for the stranger. The kettle had a cracked lid and a spout shaped like an impatient bird; it had been passed from one assistant astronomer to another for two centuries. The ritual was not kindness so much as a way of giving her hands something steady to do.
Cael told her about the south platform, the strange wind that had blown upward through the stairs, and a woman on the station clock who had asked him to carry a letter to the place where tomorrow was being kept in jars. He had laughed at the instruction then. He did not laugh now.
Cael: She said the names we lose don't disappear. They become weather. I thought she was making a joke about my profession.
Mira: She might have been warning you that a memory has weight even after you stop carrying it.
At last Mira opened the letter. It contained her father's confession and the warning in her own handwriting: DO NOT LET THEM OPEN THE EASTERN LENS. Hearing the account first did not make the confession easier; it gave her one more person who might help carry the burden of it.
!jump atlas_wakes

@scene protective_choice
!set truth += 1
!set mercy -= 1
Mira examined the wax, held it over a lamp to check for false impressions, and tested the paper against the observatory's oldest catalog. The ink proved genuine. The seal contained a fleck of blue glass from the workshop where her father had once repaired instruments, a detail no one could have forged without knowing the house where she grew up.
Cael watched without objecting. The patience was worse than suspicion; if he had been angry, Mira could have accused him of manipulation. Instead he stood with both hands visible and let her establish the truth at her own speed.
Mira: When I find out you have lied, I will have you locked in a storeroom full of failed telescopes.
Cael: Those are the only interesting kind.
She opened the letter. It named her father as an accomplice in the erasures and warned against the eastern lens. Mira laid it on the table and felt the weight of a fact that no lock could protect her from.
!jump atlas_wakes

@scene atlas_wakes
!background observatory_evening
!music threshold
!show mira troubled at left
!show cael steady at right
The atlas opened of its own accord. Its brass clasps lifted like hands recoiling from hot iron. Across the blank region where Bellwether belonged, pale lettering gathered and dissolved: a street, a garden, the beginning of a person's surname. The ink never completed a word. Each attempt left a soft sound in the room, like a match being struck in water.
Cael: Is that normal?
Mira: Until this month the book required someone to turn its pages. My definition of normal has been changing.
The blue spark she had trapped beneath her finger emerged from the paper. It floated toward the suspended globe, and the globe answered with a second spark, then a third. Six of them arranged themselves into a constellation Mira had never seen. She knew every constellation in the sky. She had also, until very recently, known every one in the atlas.
!sound bell
From the eastern wing came the sound of a door opening under enormous pressure. It was followed by a woman's voice, thin and distant, reciting the longitude of a city that no longer existed. Mira knew the voice. She had learned to name the stars by following its rhythm from one line to the next. It belonged to her mother, who had died the night her father disappeared.
Mira: That voice has been buried for seventeen years.
Cael: Then perhaps the grave isn't where we think it is.
Mira closed her eyes. She saw her mother in the kitchen, sprinkling salt on a window ledge to make the weather change. She could not remember whether the salt had worked. She had forgotten the answer long before the city began vanishing. She wondered how much of a life could be removed without leaving a blank space anyone would think to investigate.
The Council of Cartographers had forbidden the eastern wing after the catastrophe in which her parents disappeared. Their proclamation was written in language that looked impressive and meant nothing: for the continuity of public navigation, certain navigational mechanisms are to remain undisturbed until such time as they cease to pose a navigational concern. Mira had been seven. She could now translate the proclamation: we are afraid, and we would rather you be afraid of our authority than of what frightens us.
Cael: There are two ways to get to the east wing. An official staircase guarded by people who collect salaries, and the old maintenance route above the orrery. I have experience with roofs.
Mira: The atlas has a third path. It connects places by memory instead of distance. The last time I used it, I ended up somewhere I did not recognize and spent three days forgetting why I had gone.
Cael: And the safest route?
Mira: Sit here until Bellwether becomes a polite gap in somebody else's story.
They both looked down at the book. A small golden circle formed where the southern harbor should have been. As it widened, Mira saw fragments of a street beneath its surface. There was a boy with a red scarf. There was a dog sleeping in sunlight. There was a shadow on a wall shaped like a woman holding an open umbrella.
They had an evening, perhaps two, before the erased region reached the observatory itself. Mira recognized the way the map pulsed: each loss was a rehearsal for a greater one. The unseen hand would keep drawing circles until no name remained to contradict it.
? "Search the forbidden east wing for the source." -> east_route
? "Follow the atlas through the memory of Bellwether." -> memory_route
? "Go below the clouds and meet the people at risk." -> people_route

@scene east_route
!set truth += 2
!set promise = "evidence"
!background lens_corridor
!music mystery
Mira led Cael through the maintenance ladders where her father had hidden a child's height marks among the measurements of the great lenses. She had not visited the place since she was fifteen. The marks were still there, tracing her growth in pencil from the height of a brass weight to the height of a young astronomer who had begun to be angry all the time.
Above them a machine was turning without touching any of its supports. Its concentric rings moved in contrary directions, one carrying a map of stars, one carrying the words people had given those stars, one carrying only empty slots. The empty slots were more numerous than the stars. Mira climbed onto the walkway and listened through the metal with the same care she had once given her mother's footsteps on the stairs.
Cael: You seem to have excellent reasons for not having told the Council about this.
Mira: The Council invented half those reasons.
Behind the third lens she found a ledger stitched with blue thread. It recorded the beginning of the erasure as a series of intentional tests: one deserted path, one abandoned farmhouse, one settlement of thirteen families. Each test had been described as painless. The final page contained her father's signature and a note in a different hand: unexpected persistence of absence; consider wider application.
Mira: They didn't stop because it was dangerous. They stopped because the danger was too small to be useful.
She ripped the ledger free of its wire binding. They left the wing with proof that a catastrophe had not merely occurred but had been prepared. The names of thirteen families echoed inside the book like a quiet crowd waiting to be called.
!jump keepers_of_the_weather

@scene memory_route
!set wonder += 2
!set promise = "memory"
!background lost_city
!music memory
Mira put her palm on the golden circle. The atlas felt impossibly warm, as if the city beneath the paper had been holding its breath for someone to return. Cael placed his hand beside hers. For a moment the observatory was still around them. Then the sky turned inside out.
They stood on a Bellwether street paved with wet blue stone. Carts moved between lamplights. A child shouted about an escaped balloon. Nobody turned toward Mira. At the end of the road she saw the bakery's blue door and, behind it, her father kneeling to tie her shoe. He was younger than she remembered and more tired. His face was in focus. When he opened his mouth, she heard only wind.
Cael: We can see them, but we can't speak to them.
Mira: Perhaps the past does not owe us an answer.
A woman made of rain stepped out from the crowd. Her features were indistinct, and whenever Mira tried to remember them the street shifted to another afternoon. She held up a card with a precise instruction: A NAME IS NOT A PLACE. THE PERSON WHO REMEMBERS IT IS. A moment later the card became a railway ticket to a station that had been erased.
Mira took the ticket. The street folded along the seam of its railway line. They returned to the observatory with water on their boots and the smell of pears in their sleeves. The atlas had surrendered a fragment of Bellwether, but Mira's memory of her father's face was suddenly clearer. It was not healing; it was evidence that forgetting could be reversed.
!jump keepers_of_the_weather

@scene people_route
!set mercy += 2
!set bond += 1
!set promise = "witness"
!background journey_port
!music journey
They descended by the old lift to the cloudline, where the upper winds were cold enough to sing through the cables. From above, Bellwether's missing quarter had looked like a neat absence. From below it was a broken street ending in open sky, a church bell hanging without a tower, a stairwell rising to a landing where a door had never been built.
At the transfer platform below the observatory, an airship was being lashed to a pier that had no water beneath it. The dockmaster kept a ledger of arrivals and departures with the solemnity of a priest keeping a record of souls. He had learned not to erase any name, even after the passenger failed to appear. The missing travelers had accumulated across the pages until they outnumbered the living by three to one.
Cael ran his finger down the oldest column. Here was a pilot who had once taught him how to turn into a headwind without tearing the sail. Here was a cook who used to leave an extra bowl by the stove because she believed bad luck could be distracted by hospitality. Here was a woman named Nera who had climbed the tower every morning to see whether her son was among the returning ships. The letters of her name were beginning to thin. Cael asked for a pencil and darkened them until the paper grew rough.
Mira: Will that help?
Cael: I don't know. But he should not find his mother absent from the list if he comes home.
For a while they stood under the canvas awnings and watched stevedores pass a crate from hand to hand. None of them knew the destination marked on it. The crate contained shoes, winter coats, a child's patched scarf, and three books tied together with a ribbon. The address had disappeared but the objects had not. The dockmaster said this happened every week. People continued sending parcels to places that the world no longer admitted were real.
Mira realized that the dock was a museum of uncompleted acts of care. A knitted sleeve could not tell its story, but someone had made it to keep another person warm. The evidence was everywhere once she knew how to look: a bowl saved from breaking, a seat left empty, a familiar song that everyone knew the melody of but nobody could remember learning. The Council could erase the recipients from its ledgers. It could not yet erase the shape of the effort people had spent on them.
They boarded a weather skiff whose pilot was missing the right side of his memory. He knew how to steer, how to read the winds, how to navigate in darkness by the vibration of the rigging, but he could not remember where he had learned any of it. He spoke to Cael like a former friend and to Mira like a stranger. Cael accepted both forms of greeting without correction. The skiff leaned into the cloudbank, and the pier vanished behind a curtain of vapor.
!background cloud_coast
A woman in a red coat stood beside a vegetable stall. She was selling pears to people whose coins no longer bore the faces of any king. She spoke to Mira without asking who she was. Her daughter had gone to the night school on Tuesday and had not returned; on Wednesday there had been no school. By Thursday the woman no longer remembered whether she had a daughter, but each evening she set an extra place at the table because her hands kept making the movement.
Mira: What was your daughter's name?
The woman closed her eyes. She could not answer. She pointed instead to a small embroidered star on her coat, sewn with blue thread. Her daughter had made it during an illness. The woman did not know what illness, or where the bed had stood, only that the stitching had been slow and very crooked.
Cael bought the pears. He paid more than they were worth and accepted no change. Mira took down the woman's description in her ledger, from the red coat to the irregular thread. The woman watched her write as if each word were a board placed across the gap between two rooftops.
As they climbed back toward the observatory, Mira understood that the erased were not only people. They were verbs that no longer had subjects: preparing supper for, worrying about, waiting to hear from. Whatever machine could make a daughter disappear had not learned to erase a mother's habit of setting the table.
!jump keepers_of_the_weather

@scene keepers_of_the_weather
!background cloud_coast
!music journey
!show mira open at left
!show cael steady at right
The route back to the observatory crossed a shelf of cloud where the wind sounded like a hundred people whispering through their teeth. Mira had known the shelf from maps since childhood. On paper it was a pale strip between two safe crossings, with a warning that travelers should take the northern path during a storm. In reality it was a place of torn banners, old rope bridges, and bells strung between poles so far apart that no hand could have rung them. Travelers listened to the bells to determine where the air remained solid enough to carry their weight.
Cael noticed first that one of the bells had stopped ringing. He took the telescope from his belt and searched the horizon. Far out along a line of broken stone stood a small weather station, its roof sloping away toward the cloudbank. A signal lamp flashed from a window. Twice, then once, then twice again. The sequence meant distress. The nearest safe pier was almost an hour away, but the old service path ran beneath the station. It was a narrow bridge of brass grating, worn smooth by generations of maintenance crews who had crossed it carrying more confidence than equipment.
Mira: We should send word. Someone will have a rescue line.
Cael: Someone may have had a rescue line when the station was built. Look at the posts. The cables have been cut and rewound.
Mira looked again. The rope was weathered on one side and new on the other, a repair made by a person who had wanted it to pass an inspection without doing the work. Cael tied his own climbing cord around the nearest post, tested it twice, and gave her the free end. He did not say the path was safe. He asked whether she knew how to belay a rope. She said she knew the theory. He gave her a brief lesson that involved more practical advice and less reverence for the theory than she was accustomed to hearing.
They crossed one at a time. The clouds below the grating moved in the wrong direction, rising toward them as if the sky were breathing. At the midpoint the bell nearest the station began to ring despite the absence of wind. A figure appeared in the window and waved both arms. It was a girl of perhaps twelve, wearing a weather observer's coat much too large for her. The sleeves had been rolled up to the elbows. She shouted that her supervisor had gone down to the engine room and not returned. The engine had stopped at dawn. Since then, the station had been sliding away from the rest of the world.
Inside, a kettle rattled on a cold stove. The wall charts had become transparent in places. A calendar showed yesterday's date beneath tomorrow's weather forecast, but the forecast contained names rather than descriptions of cloud. The girl's own name was written in a row of careful capital letters around the doorframe, repeated so often that the ink darkened the wood. She said her name was Tessa. She had begun writing it after her supervisor disappeared, because she was afraid the station would forget her next.
Mira: How long have you been alone?
Tessa: I don't know. The clocks disagree. One says three hours. The other stopped the day my mother died. I don't remember putting that clock there.
Cael knelt by the stalled engine and opened the inspection plate. It was not an engine in the usual sense. It measured wind pressure, yes, but beneath the ordinary gears lay a ring of blue glass with the Council's eight-pointed mark. Someone had attached the weather station to the same network that was swallowing Bellwether. Its power ran through the station's old instruments, using the weather readings to conceal an accounting system that recorded the names of people passing through the cloud shelf.
The supervisor had noticed the alteration. His notebook lay beside the plate, its last entry written with such force that the pen had torn the page: THE WIND IS ONLY THE PART THEY LET US MEASURE. Beneath it, a column of names ended with Tessa's. A small circle had been drawn around her name, and the next line was blank.
Mira: He came down here to stop the machine from taking the next person. He left the chart for whoever followed him.
Cael: Can you stop it?
Mira: I can stop it making this station part of the agreement. I don't know whether the station will remain connected to the sky afterward.
Tessa had been listening from the doorway. She asked whether Mira's work would bring the supervisor back. Mira said no. Then, because the first answer had been cruelly small, she said she did not know what happened to a person taken by the network. She would not promise something that the machine had already taught her to fear.
They removed the blue-glass ring with a pair of rusted pliers and a piece of insulated wire. The moment it came free, every bell along the shelf sounded at once. The station lurched. A crack appeared across the window and widened into a line of white light. Outside, the grating bridge began to come apart at the joints. Cael shouted for Tessa to take the rope and run. She refused until Mira picked up the supervisor's notebook. The refusal cost them a few precious seconds. It also taught Mira something about the girl she would not have learned from a chart of her likely survival.
They reached the bridge as the station slid away. Cael crossed first and secured the rope to the far post. Mira tied the notebook to Tessa's coat and sent her after him. Then the grating beneath Mira's feet twisted. She lost her footing and struck the rail with her shoulder. Her atlas opened in the wind. For one impossible moment its pages held the scattered pieces of the bridge in the air as though a picture of a place could persuade the place not to fall. Cael caught her wrist and pulled. She landed on the safe side as the station vanished into cloud.
They watched the torn roof go. There was no explosion, only the soft noise of a book closing. Tessa's name remained legible on the notebook. The girl's hands shook so violently that she could not turn its pages. Mira wrapped her own coat around her and did not ask whether she was brave. Tessa had been brave. She was also cold, frightened, and furious with the world. Calling her brave would not have made any of those things disappear.
!if mercy >= 2
Mira told Tessa she could stay at the observatory until they found her relatives. The girl asked whether the observatory was safe. Mira answered that it was not, but it had people who would try to keep her alive. Tessa considered this, then nodded. She seemed to regard a truthful danger as less frightening than a promise she could not test.
!end
!if truth >= 3
The supervisor's final diagram showed three stations marked as auxiliary lenses. The locations formed a triangle around Bellwether, with the observatory at one point and AsterVale at another. The third had no label. Mira copied its coordinates into the atlas. She did not yet know where the point lay, but it meant that the erasure was not merely spreading. Someone had given it a shape.
!end
At the safe pier, Tessa asked them to write the supervisor's name on a slip of paper. She said she wanted to carry it separately from the notebook in case the ink disappeared. Mira helped her form the letters. The name was Tomas Rell. She wrote it once, then again. Cael suggested they tie the slips to different places, the way sailors tie spare lines in separate lockers so one flood cannot take them all. Tessa gave one to Mira, one to Cael, and kept the third tucked beneath her collar.
The pier's caretaker arrived with blankets and hot soup. She knew Tessa's coat but not Tessa, which frightened the girl until the woman explained that all apprentice observers wore the same faded green. They went inside to eat. Mira stood for a moment at the window and watched the empty cloud shelf. Its bells were ringing again, but without the station they sounded like a warning that had no one left to warn.
Cael: We cannot save every station along the route.
Mira: I know.
Cael: Then don't let the ones we cannot save become a reason not to remember this one.
She held the slip of paper between two fingers. The name TOMAS RELL was still there. They returned to the observatory carrying one more piece of evidence and one more person whose future could not be reduced to a number in a ledger.
!jump second_chapter

@scene second_chapter
!background observatory_night
!music archive
!show mira open at left
!show cael steady at right
By midnight the observatory had acquired a second weather. Outside, thunder rolled along the cloud shelf. Inside, a pale fog gathered on the polished marble and retreated whenever Mira spoke a name that the atlas still recognized. The six blue sparks circled the globe in the pattern of a sentence she had not learned to read.
She spread everything they had gathered across the main table: the letter from her father, the maintenance ledger or the ticket or the account of the woman in the red coat, and the list of streets she had spent weeks writing out of fear. Cael sat opposite her with his useless watch between his hands. He had taken its mechanisms apart years ago, he explained, because a watch without hands at least could not accuse him of being late.
Mira: Every route gives the same answer in a different language. Somebody made the erasure. The places haven't entirely gone. And the person who kept the machine working knew they were causing harm.
Cael: It may be worse. They may have convinced themselves the harm was mercy.
The atlas opened to a new page and displayed an image of the world as it had existed before the northern catastrophe. A coast ran into the distance where no coast could now be seen. Hundreds of islands hung like scattered petals above the water. A city larger than Bellwether occupied the center of the chart. Beside it stood the word ASTERVALE, written in the old script and surrounded by twelve compass roses. Mira had heard the name only in a children's song, in which it rhymed with 'never fail.' There were no songs in the library that actually mentioned it.
She opened the index. Someone had sewn the A pages shut. She worked the thread loose with a knife. Beneath it she found a letter her father had never sent to the Council: What we call an empty sky is a city we have chosen not to remember. AsterVale did not fall. It was exchanged. If the exchange is reversed without settling the debt, the breach will reopen and all that stands above the clouds will descend into it.
Mira: He is describing an agreement. With whom?
Cael: The sky, perhaps. It is full of people who insist on negotiating with the weather.
They searched the next pages. The account was not a natural history but the minutes of a council meeting. Seventeen years earlier, when the celestial engine had failed, the astronomers learned that a white storm would consume the island nations. An ancient instrument could prevent the storm by removing its path from the map of the world. Cities, once excluded from the storm's course, would survive as unremembered territories suspended in an invisible region beside the ordinary sky.
Cael: They called it a rescue.
Mira: A city cannot consent to a rescue in which it is not allowed to exist.
But the account did not end there. The removal had saved those cities, in a limited sense. The storm had passed over the blank places without striking them. The people inside were still alive, according to the last message received through the eastern lens. They had no memory of the world outside, and the outside world had no memory of them. They had survived by agreeing to be unfindable.
Mira: Why is Bellwether being erased now? The storm passed long ago.
A name flashed across the page: THE TOLL. The instrument had continued to take one memory from the outer world each year as payment for holding the other cities safe. The payment grew. It had begun with a child's forgotten face, a lost song, a name no longer attached to a street. Now it demanded cities. When the book reached its own maker's name, the debt would close and everything the instrument held apart would collapse into one another.
The clock struck twelve. Mira heard the first glass pane in the western corridor fracture. From the atlas came the sound of a thousand small bells ringing under water. A line of coastline disappeared. Above the cloudline, the stars were briefly arranged into the shape of a human hand.
Cael: How long?
Mira: Until dawn. Perhaps less. My father left a warning about the eastern lens, but the only way to reach AsterVale may be to open it.
Cael picked up the atlas as if it might bite him. It was heavier than the letter. His mouth tightened in a way Mira recognized from people who had decided to say something painful before they could change their minds.
Cael: I think I've been there. Not in my current life. In the part of my life that keeps being edited. I know the shape of that bridge. I know where the bakery stands. And every time I try to remember who took me across it, I lose the beginning of a different memory.
Mira: Did you carry letters between the outside and the hidden cities?
Cael: I carried messages people were told could not be delivered. I was very good at it. I think someone made me forget the recipients so that I would keep delivering.
Mira wanted to insist he was mistaken. Instead she asked him to show her his palms. On the inside of his right wrist was the faint scar of an eight-pointed compass rose, the same mark her father had used on his letters. It was not tattooed. It had been burned into the skin by something that had held him against an iron plate.
The first time she had met him, his name was the one thing she had trusted. Now it felt like a bridge that might have been built from both shores by different people.
? "Tell Cael you trust him, even with the missing memories." -> trust_courier
? "Demand that he tell you what he is hiding." -> suspect_courier
? "Stop talking and examine the compass scar for clues." -> examine_scar

@scene trust_courier
!set bond += 2
!set mercy += 1
Mira turned his hand over and closed her fingers around the scar. It was warm, not with fever but with the familiar heat of a life that had continued while the official histories were busy pretending otherwise. He had come from Bellwether carrying a letter that made his life dangerous. It was enough to begin with.
Mira: I believe you when you say you don't remember. Whatever made this mark wanted you to be ashamed of that. I won't help it.
Cael looked away. The telescopes reflected silver on his lashes. For a moment he seemed younger than he had looked on the staircase, like someone who had been carrying a secret beyond the age when a person should have to carry it alone.
Cael: I have never been offered trust without being asked for a history in exchange.
Mira: Then don't make me regret being the first.
They laughed without much sound. Somewhere above them the atlas turned another page. The laughter remained between them, and neither one tried to explain it away.
!jump ledger_of_absences

@scene suspect_courier
!set truth += 2
!set bond -= 1
Mira held the burned compass toward the lamp. He flinched when she did, a reflex too old to be performed convincingly. Her father's letter had arrived in his hands; an ancient mark lay on his skin; and the hidden city seemed to have preserved fragments of his life. The coincidences had begun to resemble a structure.
Mira: You brought me a confession from a man you say you've never met. You have the seal of his work on your wrist. Either you are lying, or somebody designed you to be the perfect messenger to a person they wanted to manipulate.
Cael: Those are not the only possibilities.
Mira: They are the ones we have time to investigate.
He told her about the dreams. A corridor of polished copper, a woman humming into a glass bowl, a small child who held his hand and called him brother. The dreams always ended with the same command: when the sky opens, do not let anyone close it again. He had hidden them because he feared they were instructions planted in him, not memories of his own.
Mira found she could be angry with him and still believe his fear was genuine. The distinction did not make her comfortable. It made her more determined to find out who had the power to make one person doubt the contents of his own mind.
!jump ledger_of_absences

@scene examine_scar
!set wonder += 2
!set truth += 1
The scar was a map in miniature. Under the observatory's longest inspection glass it resolved into a system of lines too fine for an ordinary burn: channels, bridges, twelve tiny compass stars, and a black dot where AsterVale ought to have been. A miniature of the eastern lens was written into his skin.
Mira: This is an access key. Your body remembers where the book won't.
Cael: That would explain why I never liked watches. Every watchmaker I visited insisted my pulse was out of time.
She measured the scar against the lens diagrams. Twelve concentric circles matched exactly. The key required a living messenger, not a brass mechanism, to cross from the outer world into the hidden one. Her father had known someone would be needed. Whether he had chosen Cael or tried to prevent the choice, Mira could not yet tell.
He held still while she recorded the lines. He did not ask her to apologize for treating him as evidence. When she finished, she wrote his name on a separate page and folded it into his hand.
Mira: Whatever else this mark means, this is your name. I won't allow a machine to persuade you that you don't own it.
!jump ledger_of_absences

@scene ledger_of_absences
!background observatory_night
!music memory
!show mira troubled at left
!show cael steady at right
The observatory's night watch had been abolished years earlier, but its bells still rang the rounds. Mira and Cael heard them as they crossed the empty central gallery: one for the lens chamber, two for the librarians' stair, three for the west door, four for the room in which damaged maps were kept under glass. Between the second and third bells came a pause that had not existed the previous week. Mira could remember the watchman who had complained about it. She could not remember his name. The missing interval made her feel that the building itself was learning to forget.
They carried the evidence from the night's search upstairs. A ledger, a railway ticket, or an account from a stranger on the cloud coast: each piece weighed almost nothing and refused to lie still on the table. The atlas drew them toward itself when Mira tried to set them aside. The smaller book slipped open to a page of tide measurements recorded seventeen years before the present date. Every number in the third column had been replaced by the same penciled mark. Mira recognized the shape from her father's handwriting. It meant a value had been excluded from a calculation because the person doing the calculation did not know how to measure it.
Mira: He was not hiding an error. He was hiding the things he could not count.
Cael: What if he thought he was being careful?
Mira: Then he was careful with the arithmetic and careless with the lives.
Cael offered to make tea. Mira told him there was no tea left. He opened the cupboard and found a tin with enough leaves for two cups. When she demanded to know how he had found it, he said he had looked where a person afraid of running out would hide it. The explanation irritated her because it was correct. They carried their cups into the gallery and sat beneath a painted ceiling full of constellations that no longer corresponded to the sky.
Mira's mother had painted one of those constellations herself. At the time the Council's astronomers were too proud to admit that several stars appeared in the same place on different nights. They had argued about faulty lenses, mistaken observations, and the weather. Her mother had asked what would happen if the stars were moving and the instruments remained still. She was not a Council astronomer. She was the person who cleaned the lenses when the astronomers went home. Her question had been laughed at until the new measurements came in. Then the Council published a correction without mentioning her.
The stars in the painted ceiling had not moved, but the real stars had. Mira found her mother's initials in the corner, disguised as three small dots. She had passed under them thousands of times. Tonight the ceiling looked less like a map than a record of who was allowed to name what everyone else could see.
Cael: Would she have believed the atlas could erase a city?
Mira: She believed people could do terrible things if they had written themselves permission first. The mechanism would have impressed her. The permission would not.
In the archive room, the oldest volumes were bound in red leather whose color had faded to brown. The catalog called them municipal histories. Their pages contained tax appeals, bridge repairs, disputes about fishing rights, and the inventories taken after winter storms. These were not the records a Council official would display to visiting dignitaries. They were the records of things people had complained about enough to make someone write them down. Mira had once found them dull. Now she turned page after page as if she were searching for the heartbeat of the world.
The first empty entry concerned a ferry operator who had requested compensation for a broken landing stage. The second described a school where the teacher believed her pupils were being made ill by an unknown substance in the drinking water. The third was a petition signed by nineteen residents asking that an old woman be allowed to keep her vegetable garden after the Council purchased the land beneath it. All three petitions had been rejected. None was important enough to appear in the Council's history of the northern catastrophe. Yet their names and places had faded from every subsequent register. Someone had begun testing the machinery long before any city vanished.
Mira wrote the dates in her ledger. She drew a line from the ferry operator to the school, another from the school to the garden, and at the end of the line a circle where Bellwether should have stood. The first experiments had not taken place in empty spaces. They had taken place where the Council believed nobody would notice the cost.
!if truth >= 3
Mira recognized a signature on the earliest petition: the same blue flourish she had seen beneath her father's maintenance note. He had witnessed the original test and signed not as a technician but as an approving officer. She copied the signature even though she knew it would appear in her dreams. Knowledge made her angrier than ignorance had. It also made her less able to imagine that returning to ignorance would improve the world.
!end
!if wonder >= 2
A thin sheet of light lifted from the atlas and settled on the petition about the school. For one instant Mira heard the pupils reciting the names of rivers. She could not see their faces. She wrote the rivers down anyway. The spark hovering above her shoulder grew brighter as she spoke, as if something in the machinery were registering that a sound had survived its source.
!end
!if mercy >= 2
The petition about the old woman's garden had a drawing attached. It showed a row of beans supported by four crooked sticks and a small wooden seat beneath a tree. The woman's handwriting was uneven, but the plan was careful. She had marked where her husband liked to sit in the afternoon. Mira put that drawing ahead of the calculations. It was the first record that told her exactly what a person had hoped to keep.
!end
For a time they worked without speaking. Cael read the petitioners' names aloud while Mira marked the dates. When he came to one that dissolved between his first syllable and his last, he began again. He pronounced the imperfect surviving sound, then the sound he remembered, then the silence. Mira wrote all three. Neither knew whether the act would matter. The repetition mattered to them.
Cael had learned to repair sails as an apprentice on the upper routes. He told her that a seam did not hold because the thread was strong; it held because a series of small stitches distributed the strain across cloth that would otherwise tear. A careless repair could look perfect in harbor and split the first time the wind changed. He had once admired the men who could make repairs invisible. He had since learned to admire those who left a little colored thread so the next crew knew where the weakness had been.
Mira: You're telling me to make the repair visible.
Cael: I'm telling you that someone has to know where it might break again.
Mira thought of the monument in the eastern corridor, the scratched-out stars, the official history that gave every vanished person a place in a calculation and none in a sentence. The Council's greatest achievement had been to make its repairs invisible. The people who lived in the repaired world had been forbidden to see the stitches. No wonder the whole thing was coming apart.
They found a drawer labeled RESTITUTION. It held three coins, a key, a child's brass whistle, and a list of people to whom the objects should be returned when it became possible to find them. The oldest entry was ninety-four years old. Beneath it, in the same careful hand, was a note: NO AMOUNT PAID. The ledger had survived because no clerk knew whether to classify the restitution as complete or pending. Mira put the coins back and took the whistle. She would carry it until she could find its owner, or someone who knew the owner's name.
As they left, Cael paused before the doors to the forbidden corridor. His scar had begun to glow so faintly that Mira could see it only when he turned his wrist toward the dark. He said he had been here before. He did not mean the observatory; he meant this particular door, with its brass lock shaped like two hands that would not touch. In his memory someone had said that the door would open only for a person who had forgotten what lay beyond it. He could not remember whether the voice had been a warning or an invitation.
Mira: You don't have to come through.
Cael: I have been avoiding a forgotten place for so long that I have almost forgotten why. If you're willing to open it, I would rather know what I was afraid of.
She turned the key. The lock opened without resistance. A damp draft flowed out carrying the smell of a summer street after rain. For a heartbeat Mira imagined that Bellwether had returned and was waiting just beyond the threshold, ordinary as the day she last saw it. Then the draft changed, and she smelled old glass and the electricity of an instrument left running long after its makers were gone. They entered together.
!jump third_chapter

@scene third_chapter
!background lens_corridor
!music mystery
!panel default
The forbidden corridor smelled of old rain and electrical dust. Once, every room in this wing had contained a practical object: an instrument to measure the tilt of a planet, a clock that could mark the passing of clouds, a map that answered questions about roads. Now each door was marked with a star, and each star had been scratched out. The absence of the stars unsettled Mira more than the locks. Someone had taken care not to leave a symbol by which the rooms might be remembered.
They passed a gallery of bronze busts, each covered by a dark cloth. Mira lifted the corner of one. The face beneath had been worn smooth, as if by centuries of weather, although the sculptor's date was only twenty-three years ago. A plaque at the base read: FIRST DIRECTOR OF THE HELD CITY PROJECT. The director's name was gone.
Cael: It is possible to build a monument without understanding whom you are honoring.
Mira: That is what most monuments are.
The corridor narrowed into a passage made of glass. Clouds pressed against the walls. Their reflections turned Mira and Cael into a procession: two people followed by other versions of themselves, one older, one younger, one carrying a child, one who had never met the other. The reflections moved out of order. In one pane Cael reached for Mira's hand before she stumbled; in another she was already falling. The future was as restless as the past.
Mira walked faster. She refused to look at the reflection in which her father appeared beside her. He wore the blue waistcoat he had worn on the day he vanished and was looking not at the lens but at her, with the exhausted tenderness of a man who had spent his last hope badly.
At the end of the passage stood the eastern lens. It was enormous: a clear sphere held in twelve great rings, suspended over a pit that appeared to descend into the night beyond the world. Blue fires burned around its rim. Across its surface, innumerable names were engraved in letters as small as the grains of an old photograph.
A woman was standing on the opposite side of the sphere. Her coat was the color of fog, her hair white, her body indistinct at the edges. Mira knew her from a picture in the kitchen; in the picture she was holding a blue bowl and laughing because something beyond the frame had made her drop a spoon.
Mira: Mother?
The woman did not move. Her voice came from the glass, not her throat.
Voice: The lens is not a door. It is an agreement made visible. If you open it without learning the terms, the city you love will become the price of the city you save.
Mira stepped forward. Her hand passed through a patch of cold light. The figure turned into tiny stars, then reassembled at the far side of the lens, nearer than before.
Mira: Are you alive?
Voice: There are questions a person may answer only by choosing what they mean by the word. I am what the lens remembered of your mother. I am also a message she left in case you came here before the hour of the closing.
Mira: Did she leave me anything else?
Voice: She left you the belief that a daughter might become wiser than the people who were meant to keep her safe.
The words were terrible in their smallness. Mira had prepared for a warning, a confession, perhaps an indictment. She had not prepared to be spoken to as a daughter by a shape that was almost her mother. She knew the difference between a memory and a person. It did not lessen her wish to be held.
Cael: We have not come to break the lens. We want to know what was promised, and to whom.
Voice: Then you must read the three records. The First contains the contract. The Second contains the names the Council removed from it. The Third contains the witness who could make the contract binding again. You cannot bring all three records through the passage intact. It must be decided which loss you are willing to accept.
Behind the woman, three doors opened onto impossible spaces: a room of burning ledgers, a garden where every flower carried a name, and an observatory seen from beneath an ocean. Mira understood that the choices represented different kinds of evidence. She also understood that every choice would leave something unknown.
? "Take the contract and learn the exact terms." -> record_contract
? "Take the names and protect those who cannot speak." -> record_names
? "Find the living witness who can overturn the bargain." -> record_witness

@scene record_contract
!set truth += 3
!set debt = "contract"
!background archive_fire
!music threshold
The burning room was not hot. Each book flamed with a color like pale green glass, and each page burned only while someone read it. Mira opened the largest volume and found the first signatures: the Council of Cartographers, the chief astronomer, and the Keeper of the Threshold, who had no hand with which to write. Under the signatures was a clause no one had ever quoted publicly: the instrument shall preserve the chosen cities beyond the reach of the storm, while collecting from the world a proportionate remembrance of all it has spared.
There was a proviso. The debt could be forgiven if the people of the hidden cities themselves chose to reopen the passage, accepting the return of the storm with it. No outside authority was permitted to make that choice on their behalf. The Council had hidden this clause. They had continued paying in the names of other people rather than offering the rescued cities a chance to decide.
Cael: They knew there was a lawful way out.
Mira: They were not willing to let the people whose lives were affected have the final word.
The book closed as the fire reached its binding. Mira carried a single charred page back to the lens, the condition written on it in brass-colored ink. The other doors were gone. She had gained a right and lost the names of those who might exercise it.
!jump chamber_of_measures

@scene record_names
!set mercy += 3
!set debt = "names"
!background name_garden
!music memory
The garden held no birds. Its leaves moved in a wind Mira could not feel, and each flower bloomed only when a name was spoken aloud. Some flowers were immense and bright; others were small enough to fit beneath her thumbnail. There were millions. The paths were laid out in the shape of a language no one in the observatory had taught her.
At the center stood a bench occupied by an old woman sewing a star onto a red coat. When Mira approached, the woman looked up with the patience of someone who had been waiting for a stranger in a dream. She gave Mira a list of names: daughters and sons, bakers and astronomers, the keeper of a ferry, a boy who had written three dreadful poems and believed one of them was excellent. Each name felt heavier than the last.
Old Woman: Tell them that we existed. If you cannot bring us home, do not let us become the price of forgetting another person.
Mira wrote until her fingers cramped. Cael helped by holding the pages steady as the garden began to fade. She carried the list back to the lens, rich in names and without the precise words of the bargain. She had chosen the witnesses over the law.
!jump chamber_of_measures

@scene record_witness
!set wonder += 3
!set debt = "witness"
!background undersea_lens
!music mystery
They descended into an observatory beneath a black sea. Fish moved between the bookshelves, as if the books had grown there instead of being placed by human hands. In the center hung a living bell. Its rim rose and fell like a sleeping chest. When Mira touched it, the water became a room full of voices.
The Keeper of the Threshold appeared not as a monster but as a child in a silver coat, no older than seven. Its eyes held the patience of something that had waited beyond the age of stars. It had witnessed the first bargain. It had not been permitted to record a dissent, because the language of the contract contained no provision for a witness to disagree.
Keeper: I remember those whom the Council did not ask. I cannot decide for them. But I can carry their answers across the boundary, if someone returns me to the place where I was made.
Cael: And where is that?
Keeper: In the heart of the instrument. Where all erased names go until the world learns to speak them again.
Mira carried the bell's small silver clapper back to the lens. She had found a witness, but not the words of the bargain or the citizens' names. The omission felt dangerous. It was also the shape of a hope.
!jump chamber_of_measures

@scene chamber_of_measures
!background undersea_lens
!music mystery
!show mira troubled at left
!show cael wary at right
The three records converged on a room beneath the eastern lens. Its ceiling was made of curved glass, and above the glass something that resembled water moved slowly against gravity. Tiny points of blue light drifted through it. Mira knew the technical explanation: the chamber was a pressure-balancing cavity connected to the lens. Her father had designed it to demonstrate that a sealed system could hold a force without allowing the force to escape. The resemblance to an ocean was a coincidence. It frightened her anyway.
The room contained twelve chairs and a long table. Each chair bore the name of a Council minister on a brass plaque. The names were still present; nobody had tried to erase them. The table was marked with a grid of numbers, one for every place the machine had been ordered to preserve. Mira ran her hand across the grid and found that some squares were warmer than others. In the warmer squares were tiny paper slips, folded so tightly she had to use the tip of her instrument to open them. They listed costs: an orchard exchanged for a bridge, a row of houses for a hospital wing, a railway platform for a winter without famine. At the bottom of each slip was a signature.
Cael: They made the losses look like payments.
Mira: Payments made by people who were never told they owed anything.
She unfolded the oldest slip. It concerned a fishing village whose inhabitants had been moved inland during a storm. The Council's report said the transfer had saved everyone. An annotation beneath the number of survivors described the cost as a minor displacement. Mira turned the paper over. On the back, in an unfamiliar hand, someone had written that the village's graveyard had remained on the coast, and the families had been forbidden to return to it. The Council counted lives saved. The families counted the names of their dead. Both numbers were true. Only one had been permitted to govern what happened next.
Cael examined the empty place at the end of the table. There was no chair there, only a circle cut into the floor where something heavy had once rested. The groove was wide enough to hold a person. A brass rail ran from the circle to the lens above. He recognized the arrangement from the old courier stations, where a message could be sent through a vacuum tube by placing it inside a sealed carrier. The Council had built a carrier large enough for a human body.
He put one boot on the circle and felt the scar on his wrist grow hot. The glass above them brightened. A voice came from the water, quiet and precise: SUBJECT RECOGNIZED. TRANSIT AUTHORITY INCOMPLETE. Mira pulled him back before he could step farther in. The circle went dark, but the voice had told them something no ledger contained. Cael had been registered as a traveler whose permission was required for the system to operate. His forgetting had not made his authority disappear. It had simply made him unable to exercise it.
Mira: You were not only a messenger. They made you part of the mechanism.
Cael: Then why do I remember only the journeys?
Mira: Because someone needed you to keep traveling, not to understand why.
He sat in the nearest chair, a minister's name beneath his hands. Mira wanted to tell him this made him important. She recognized the cruelty of that comfort before she spoke. It meant he had been used. His place in the machine was not proof that the people who built it valued him. It was proof that they valued what they could make him do.
They found a cabinet of sealed testimony. Each drawer contained a statement given during the first months after the catastrophe. Some witnesses described an extraordinary storm. Others described the smell of bread in a city they could no longer reach. One person said the boundary had been erected to protect children from a disease; another said the disease had appeared only after the boundary. The statements contradicted one another in details. They agreed about the fear. The Council's published account had removed the contradictions and all mention of the Council's own uncertainty.
Mira: If we take this to the city, they will disagree about what it proves.
Cael: They already disagree. The difference is that they might finally be allowed to disagree about the evidence instead of about the rumor of it.
The glass ceiling shuddered. A dark figure appeared beyond the blue water, too distant for them to see its face. It raised one hand. A line of text formed across the lens: THE SYSTEM HAS NEVER CLAIMED TO BE JUST. The figure lowered its hand. Another line appeared: IT CLAIMS TO PREVENT A WORSE OUTCOME. Mira stared until the letters began to drift apart. It was the defense the Council had offered for every action whose consequences it wished to conceal.
!if truth >= 4
Mira found the date of the last recalculation. It had been made only three days earlier. The Council had not lost control of the machine by accident; someone was still entering revised values while the city disappeared. The ink on the paper had barely dried. The evidence meant they were not dealing only with the mistakes of the dead. They were confronting people who knew enough to stop and had chosen to continue.
!end
!if wonder >= 3
A blue spark passed through the ceiling and settled on the testimony from a child who had described the storm as a living animal. For one instant the ink stood upright on the page like grass. Mira could read a sentence that had been scratched out: IT WAS ASKING US TO REMEMBER. She did not know whether the child's testimony had been literal. She knew a machine that required forgetting might have reasons to fear a child who described the world differently.
!end
!if bond >= 2
Cael held out his hand. Mira took it. He had no useful memory to offer her, no technical knowledge that would repair the mechanism in a single inspired gesture. What he could offer was the willingness to remain present in a room designed to make his presence an instrument. For a moment the scar dimmed. The carrier circle did not recognize the gesture as an authorized command. Mira found that she was glad.
!end
At the far wall hung a framed map of Bellwether made from hundreds of narrow metal strips. Each strip carried the name of a road. The strips could be moved to simulate the city under different conditions: a flood, a fire, a population increase, a damaged bridge. Mira had studied the model in her childhood and admired its precision. Now she noticed that no strip represented the people who lived along those roads. The city was modeled as a set of routes through which supplies could pass. It had no room for a person sitting beside a window while waiting for a friend.
She began removing the strips. Cael helped without asking what pattern she intended to make. They laid the metal on the floor in the order of the stories they had heard: the bakery with the blue door, the station whose platform ended in the sea, the garden with the crooked sticks, the ferry operator who had wanted a safe landing, the woman who had sent a letter through a broken passage. The new arrangement was not a map a navigator could use. It was a map of reasons people might want to return.
As the last strip fell into place, the light behind the ceiling shifted. The water became clear. For one heartbeat they could see AsterVale beneath it: not an image projected by the lens but a real city observed from an impossible angle. A person leaned out of a window to call a child home. The call sounded distant and imperfect, and neither Mira nor Cael could understand the name. They listened until the image vanished.
Mira knew she could not carry the whole table, all the testimony, and the metal strips into the chamber above. She chose the few pieces that showed how the calculation had been made and the stories it had excluded. Cael put the remaining testimony in the center of the table where it would be visible to whoever entered next. He turned every minister's chair toward it. It was a small act of impertinence. Mira thought her mother would have approved.
They climbed the narrow stair toward the storm. Above them, the glass continued to hold its impossible ocean, but the currents had changed direction. The chamber was beginning to respond to a map on which people occupied more space than the machine had intended. Mira could not decide whether the change meant they were succeeding or whether they had awakened something that had been sleeping for seventeen years. She went upward anyway.
!jump fourth_chapter

@scene fourth_chapter
!background observatory_storm
!music storm
!show mira troubled at left
!show cael wary at right
The storm began at the wrong end of the sky. Lightning rose out of the clouds and vanished into the stars without striking the observatory; thunder arrived before its light. Every window flashed with a different image of the city that had been erased. In one pane Bellwether was burning. In another it had never been built. In a third, small children were playing a game of marbles in streets that still existed, oblivious to the fact that their existence was being argued over by people who were not present.
Mira laid her chosen evidence on the floor beneath the eastern lens. The sphere brightened. For the first time she could see beyond the glass: a market, an avenue of cypress trees, and a canal illuminated by a thousand floating candles. AsterVale was beautiful and entirely ordinary in the ways that mattered. Its people were hurrying home before the weather, losing gloves, finding missing coins, quarreling over the price of fruit. The Council's histories had spoken of it as an abstract quantity, an instrument's burden. It was impossible to watch the people crossing the square without recognizing how much that description had concealed.
Cael pressed his hand against the glass. His scar answered with a blue pulse, like an artery transmitting light. For an instant the surface became soft. He recoiled, then pressed again. Mira saw a girl on the other side, perhaps twelve years old, wearing the same blue scarf she had seen in the fragment of Bellwether. She raised her hand to meet his, but the glass held a distance neither of them could measure.
Cael: I know her.
Mira: From where?
Cael: I know the way she frowns when she is trying to remember the name of a bird. I know the first word she ever said, and that it was not a word at all, only a sound we all pretended to understand. I know she used to draw windows on the inside of boxes so that things put away would not become lonely.
He stepped back. His face had gone pale. The girl on the other side was turning away, called by a woman from somewhere behind the market.
Cael: She's my sister. I had a sister.
Mira had seen him smile in the early evening. Now she watched the exact moment a man discovered that the space left by a person was larger than his life. She reached for him, then stopped when she saw the way his fingers were shaking.
Cael: Her name is Elian. It was Elian. I do not know whether it still is.
Mira: We will find out.
He looked at her with sudden fear, not for himself but for the promise she had made. It was easy to say they would find someone who had been removed from the world. Such promises could become a new way of being cruel.
!sound bell
An immense shadow crossed the glass. The market disappeared. The Council's seal rose in its place, followed by a figure made of gold lines: Chancellor Orsol, director of the observatory and presiding cartographer of the upper islands. She had taught Mira mathematics when she was a child. Later, when Mira asked where her father had gone, Orsol answered with the phrase all powerful people use when they have decided a child will not remember the answer: one day you will understand.
Orsol: Mira Vale. You have opened a prohibited instrument and disturbed the agreement that keeps these islands safe. I expected better of you.
Mira: I expected the same of you, Chancellor. We seem to be having a disappointing evening.
Orsol: Your father's actions were necessary. He understood what you have not yet learned: a disaster averted is invisible evidence. You cannot point to the people saved from a storm that never struck and demand to see their gratitude.
Mira: You can ask them whether they consent to being kept outside the world. You never did.
Orsol: If the hidden cities choose to return, the storm returns with them. The outer islands will fall. Their children will drown in the clouds. This is the weight on the other side of your argument.
Mira: Then tell the people the truth and allow them to choose with it.
Orsol: Truth is a luxury when one has the time to debate. We have less than four hours. Close the lens. Give me the atlas. I will renegotiate the toll and delay the closing for another generation.
The offer sounded rational in exactly the way dangerous things often do when someone has carefully removed the part of the problem that cannot be solved. Another generation meant another century of missing families, erased schools, and mothers setting places for daughters whose names were gone. The Chancellor had lived long enough to believe that postponement was a form of mercy.
Cael: If you know the hidden city is alive, why did you tell me that the voices I heard there were dreams?
Orsol looked at him as if seeing an inconvenient detail in an old equation.
Orsol: Because a messenger who remembers where he has been cannot be safely sent back. Your mind was not strong enough to carry the boundary. We spared you the burden.
Cael: You took my sister and called it mercy.
Orsol: I kept her alive.
The silence after that was more frightening than the storm. Mira understood that both claims could be true and that the truth of one did not cancel the violence of the other. The atlas was not an ethical machine. It was a machine in a world where ethical choices had been withheld until they resembled mechanical necessities.
Orsol: I will be at the eastern stair when the last bell sounds. If you are wise, you will be there before me.
The seal collapsed into a circle of blue light. The market returned. Elian was gone. Cael stood very still beside the glass, and Mira felt how little use it was to be right about a question when someone had just been given back a grief he had not known he possessed.
? "Take the Chancellor's offer seriously: seek a negotiated delay." -> negotiate_route
? "Enter the hidden city and seek its people's consent." -> consent_route
? "Break the Council's grip and expose the concealed history." -> expose_route

@scene negotiate_route
!set mercy += 1
!set truth += 1
!set witness = "parley"
Mira pulled a chair to the eastern lens and sat down as if she were preparing for a difficult faculty meeting. She took out clean paper, a dry pen, and three empty columns. At the top of the first she wrote THE CITY. At the top of the second THE OUTER ISLANDS. At the top of the third WHAT NEITHER PARTY HAS AGREED TO LOSE.
Cael: Do you believe Orsol will negotiate?
Mira: I believe she will bargain. That is not the same thing.
Mira asked the lens to transmit a proposal to the hidden city: an emergency delegation of residents, selected without the Council's approval, to meet in the threshold before the closing. She included the disputed clause from the contract if she had recovered it; otherwise she sent the list of names or the witness's silver clapper as proof that the outer world had begun listening. The glass trembled. A reply came in writing so pale that Cael had to hold a lamp beneath it.
The people of AsterVale were willing to send representatives. They required that Orsol remove the restrictions on speaking of the missing cities and that the outer islands recognize their right to refuse the agreement. The Chancellor would not like the terms. Mira liked them rather more than she expected.
She sent the invitation to Orsol without asking permission. The political act was small: a letter written in a room, a name placed beside another name. It was also the first decision about the hidden cities made by someone other than the people who had hidden them. Mira felt the irony and hoped the delegates would forgive her for it.
!jump crossing_the_seam

@scene consent_route
!set mercy += 2
!set bond += 1
!set witness = "city"
Cael touched the scar on his wrist. The lines glowed beneath his skin. When he set his hand against the glass, the lens changed from a barrier into a reflection. Mira saw herself on the other side, holding his hand as if they had already made the journey. He asked whether she was prepared to discover that the city might choose to stay hidden, even if it meant Bellwether would continue to disappear.
Mira: If we ask people what they want only when we expect them to agree with us, we have not asked them at all.
The passage opened around them. There was no rushing wind or spectacle of broken stars. They simply walked into a room that had been waiting for someone to open a door. AsterVale smelled of rain on stone and fresh bread. A woman carrying a basket went past without noticing the impossible arrival of two strangers. Mira was grateful for the indifference; she had had enough of becoming an event in other people's lives.
The city would speak for itself, and their job would be to listen even if its answer frightened them.
!jump crossing_the_seam

@scene expose_route
!set truth += 3
!set bond += 1
!set witness = "public"
Mira connected the eastern lens to the public signal mirrors that had once carried weather reports to every island above the clouds. She had repaired them as a girl. The Chancellor had banned their use when the erasures began, describing public observation as an unnecessary source of alarm.
Cael: How many people will see this?
Mira: Everyone who still owns a mirror that faces east.
She transmitted the ledger of tests, the confession from her father, the name of AsterVale, and every remembered fragment of Bellwether. In distant windows the light changed. Households paused over breakfast tables that had become the wrong shape. The voice of the city reached them as a thin music under the ordinary noise of life.
Orsol's seal appeared, attempting to block the transmission. Mira had already sent it through the old lighthouse system, whose mirrors were too simple to recognize an order from the Council.
For the first time the people of the outer world were permitted to know what had been done in their names. The knowledge did not tell them what to choose. It made the choice their own problem, which was more than the Council had ever allowed.
!jump crossing_the_seam

@scene crossing_the_seam
!background lost_city
!music journey
!show mira open at left
!show cael steady at right
The eastern lens did not open like a door. It became a distance. One moment Mira stood on the marble floor of the observatory with the storm against the windows, and the next the air between her and the market had lengthened into a corridor of pale blue weather. The corridor had no walls. Along its sides hung fragments of places that had been taken from the world: a bridge ending above a river with no name, a bakery window where bread cooled without an oven, a child's shoes beside a staircase that went upward forever. The objects did not move when she approached. They seemed to be holding still to avoid being noticed.
Cael tested the first step with the caution of a sailor placing his foot on a gangplank that had broken under him before. His scar shone beneath his cuff. Each step drew an echo from a different direction. Mira heard her father's voice explaining how to read the tides, then a woman's laugh she had not thought about for years, then the sound of a tram bell in Bellwether. She knew the last sound was real. She could no longer decide whether the other two were memories or something the lens had made from her longing.
Mira: If it shows us what we want, we must not follow it blindly.
Cael: It isn't showing me what I want.
He was looking at a small room suspended above the corridor, lit by a single oil lamp. A coat hung beside the door. On the table lay two cups, one with a chipped rim. The room was so ordinary that Mira almost missed it. Cael had stopped breathing. He said a person had once waited for him in that room and he had promised to return before the lamp went out. He could not recall the person's face. He could remember the particular shame of making a promise he had not been able to keep.
There was no safe way to pass the room. The corridor curved toward it, following some rule of memory rather than geometry. Mira wanted to hurry him onward. Then she remembered the dockmaster darkening names on an arrival register and the child who had wound a mechanical bird three times before its message returned. Perhaps the corridor was asking them to look at what they had tried to ignore. Or perhaps the corridor was merely cruel. The machine had never been required to distinguish between those possibilities.
They entered the room. It was empty except for the cups and the lamp. On the mantel lay a collection of postcards showing places Mira had never visited. A seaside town was painted in blue. A narrow valley held a bridge that seemed too delicate to bear a cart. A lighthouse stood on a cliff above water the color of iron. The messages on the backs were written in several hands, all addressed to a person named simply HOME. Someone had collected the cards not to preserve geography but to preserve the hope that the sender might be understood.
Cael took the chipped cup. It was warm. He said he remembered a morning on a ship when he had burned his hand carrying tea through a storm. A person with dark hair had laughed at him and taken the cup away before he could drop it. He did not know who the person was, but he remembered that laughter had made the storm less frightening. The room offered him no answer beyond the fragment. Mira let the silence remain.
!if bond >= 2
Mira took the unchipped cup and sat across from him. They drank the last warmth from an imaginary breakfast. No one told a story, and no one tried to make a meaning out of the empty chair between them. Cael's shoulders loosened for the first time since he entered the lens. When they rose, he set both cups on the table and straightened the crooked lamp. Mira understood that trust could consist of allowing an unfinished memory to remain unfinished without insisting that the other person fill it for you.
!end
!if bond < 2
Mira stayed by the door while Cael examined the room. She knew he might read her distance as suspicion. She also knew that trust could not be manufactured out of urgency. When he returned, she asked whether he wanted to speak. He shook his head. They continued with a little more space between them, but he did not leave her behind.
!end
Outside, the corridor was changing. The fragments of lost places had begun to turn toward AsterVale like flowers toward a sun. The city's lamps shone at the far end, visible through a veil of rain. For the first time Mira noticed footprints on the pale floor. Some were enormous; some belonged to children. The prints came from the direction of the market and stopped here, as though a thousand people had tried to leave and encountered a limit they could not name.
Mira crouched to examine the nearest print. Its outline was smudged by the marks of a hand. Someone had knelt beside it, trying to find a way to lift the person who had been stopped. A button lay in the center, blue and worn almost smooth. She recognized the type from coats sold in the city's northern quarter before the Council closed the passage. She put it in her pocket beside the brass whistle. Evidence was accumulating around her in the shapes of things people had touched.
The machine spoke without words. The air thickened, and suddenly she knew the price the old agreement demanded. To cross the passage, a traveler must surrender an exact memory of the person whose return they most desired. The memory was not destroyed immediately. It became part of the energy that held the boundary closed. Travelers had been paying the cost for generations without knowing there was a toll. The city could welcome them; the world would gradually make them unable to find it again.
Cael: Is that what happened to me?
Mira: It may be part of it. The passage takes the thing most likely to bring you back.
Cael looked toward the lamps. His expression did not change, but he took the old watch from his coat and placed it in Mira's hand. He asked her to remember that it had belonged to him, even if he forgot the reason he had carried it. He did not ask her to tell him what he would be losing. There were some negotiations a person could not conduct without knowing enough to refuse.
Mira turned the watch over. On the back, beneath the scratches of years, was a tiny engraving of a bird in flight. It was the same shape as the brass whistle in her pocket. Both might have come from the same workshop. She wondered how many people had carried pieces of the same vanished world without recognizing them.
Mira: We won't pay the toll.
Cael: Then how do we cross?
Mira: We make the passage remember that we have refused.
She opened the atlas and wrote the sentence in her own ledger before speaking it aloud. The letters shone against the blue floor. A second set appeared beside them in Cael's hand, though he had not held a pen. The corridor trembled. For a moment every fragment of place seemed to lean toward the words, and Mira saw how thoroughly the old agreement had depended on travelers accepting costs they did not know they were paying.
The lens did not shatter. It did something harder. It hesitated. The distance to the city shortened by one small step. Mira took that step without surrendering the watch. Cael followed. Above them, the first thunder of the storm sounded like a door being closed somewhere far away. The corridor lengthened again, then shortened, then began to dissolve into air that smelled of oranges and coal smoke. They emerged under an arch whose stones were covered with the names of people who had tried to return.
!if promise == "evidence"
The ledger from the eastern wing grew warm beneath Mira's arm. Its pages opened to the names of the first families selected for the experiments. The arch seemed to recognize the record as an accusation. Several of the oldest carved names brightened, and Mira understood that proof could become a key when someone had tried to make ignorance a lock.
!end
!if promise == "memory"
The railway ticket from Bellwether unfolded in her pocket. Across its blank surface appeared a destination in pale blue ink: ASTERVALE, VIA THE LONG WAY HOME. The arch answered with the sound of wheels passing over rails. Cael smiled despite himself, as though the world had briefly remembered a joke he had once enjoyed.
!end
!if promise == "witness"
A gust carried the scent of pears from the cloud coast. Mira remembered the woman in the red coat and her embroidered star. For an instant she felt a pressure at her elbow, the warmth of a person guiding her through a crowded street. No one was there. She decided that an absent person might still be able to lead the living, provided the living were willing to remember what she had asked of them.
!end
Before them lay AsterVale. It was neither paradise nor prison in the theatrical sense Mira had expected. A delivery cart had overturned in the street, and two children were trying to help its driver retrieve a scattered load of onions. A woman scolded them for getting under the wheels. The driver thanked them anyway. A city that could still be irritated about onions, Mira thought, had not entirely surrendered to the terms of its captivity.
!jump threshold_market

@scene threshold_market
!background hidden_market
!music journey
!show mira open at left
!show cael steady at right
The hidden city began where the eastern lens ended. Its central market occupied a wide circle beneath towers built from gold limestone, each connected to its neighbor by a bridge of dark blue metal. Lanterns hung from cables above the stalls. Every lantern contained the name of a person who had left the city, as if the people had tried to send their absent loved ones a light without knowing whether a light could cross the boundary.
Mira entered the market by following the smell of oranges. It surprised her that the smell was ordinary. She had expected the air of a hidden city to feel enchanted or poisoned or at least different in a way a careful observer could describe. Instead the fruit was a little bruised, the stalls were badly balanced, and a man with a remarkable mustache had arranged his oranges in a pyramid that was already leaning toward collapse. One fell. A girl caught it, tossed it back, and received an insult from the vendor that made them both laugh. Life had not become more symbolic simply because someone had made it secret.
A printer's shop stood under the southern bridge. Its owner, a woman named Ilya, was setting type for a newspaper that could not leave the city. Every day she printed a page headed WHAT WE STILL KNOW. Under the heading were corrections, births, deaths, the locations of newly opened clinics, and a column for memories people were afraid of losing. The column had once held notices about lost umbrellas and broken engagements. Now it took up most of the page. A parent described the smell of a baby's hair; an apprentice recorded the sound his master's cough made when a joke was particularly bad; a musician wrote that the third note of her mother's favorite tune should be played a fraction too early.
Ilya told Mira that she had been accused of sentimentalism by the city government. She pointed to the wall of proofs behind her, where the editions hung one over another like an uneven tapestry.
Ilya's last issue had printed a map of streets that no longer led anywhere. A month before that, she had published the names of three men who worked at the boundary and were lost during a night shift. The official report described the incident as a disruption of access. The newspaper described their families. The two accounts were not contradictory, Ilya said. One was simply smaller than the truth.
Mira: Do the people read it?
Ilya: Some read it. Some burn it to keep warm. I think both are reasons to print another copy.
Cael noticed a sheet hanging near the press and stopped. It showed an engraving of a ship approaching AsterVale from the upper cloudline. The lettering beneath had been cut away, but he recognized the rigging. He had sailed on the ship. In the engraving someone stood at the stern, one hand raised in farewell. He could not see her face, but he knew how that person leaned when the wind came from the east. For a moment his composure fell from him entirely. Mira did not ask for an explanation. She took the image down and paid for it.
They carried the engraving through streets bright with laundry, past a school where children were drawing maps of their bedrooms in order to learn what the word geography had once included. In one map the bed occupied the whole page. In another the doorway led directly to the moon. The teacher had written beneath each drawing: A PLACE IS NOT ONLY WHERE IT IS. It was a simple sentence. Mira wanted to return to the observatory and place it beside every theorem her father had ever written.
Under the western bridge they found a shop repairing mechanical birds. Each bird contained a tiny folded paper on which a patron could write a name. When wound, it would fly a short, uncertain circle and return to the hand that released it. Mira watched a boy set one loose over the canal. It landed on the wrong balcony. The boy laughed, tried again, and on the third attempt the bird returned to him. The message inside read: COME HOME WHEN YOU CAN. There was no address. There never had been.
The city, she thought, had kept inventing ways to send love across an absence. These inventions would be called inefficient by a machine designed to preserve exact quantities. They were, to Mira, the strongest evidence that the machine had never understood what it was preserving.
Vendors sold the ordinary necessities of life: tea, flour, shoelaces, maps of streets that ended at the city's invisible border. The people had adapted to their confinement with the inventiveness that makes it possible to live inside almost any injustice. Children had made a game of throwing stones against the glass wall and watching them return, older than they had been when they left a hand. The bakers traded recipes from memory because none of their old books could be read by anyone outside the boundary.
Mira stood near a fountain whose water flowed upward and read a notice on the town board. It announced a missing person's meeting, a performance by the winter choir, and the selection of delegates to the Emergency Assembly. The last announcement was written in nervous handwriting, as though the town clerk had not expected the word emergency to become an everyday part of civic life.
Cael was searching every face. Mira did not need to ask whom he hoped to find. He had not spoken Elian's name since Orsol accused him of being unfit to remember her. He touched the leather strap of his satchel whenever someone with a blue scarf passed.
Mira: There are thousands of people here. We will have to search with more than our eyes.
Cael: I remember where she used to sit when we bought pastries. If there is a street with a clock that runs backward, the shop may still be there.
The clock ran backward. A small boy was winding it from a ladder, laughing because he had been told the clock had always done that and saw no reason it should change. A woman behind a counter recognized Cael's scar before she recognized his face. She put down a tray of sugared pears and began to cry with the calm certainty of someone who had waited too long to be surprised.
Baker: You're one of the messengers. We were told you could come back only if the cities agreed to forget you.
Cael: I didn't agree to forget anyone.
Baker: It is possible that the people who signed for you did not ask.
She told them that Elian had been taken to the House of Returns, a school for children whose families were outside the boundary. The children were taught the names of cities they could not reach, but not the faces of the people who had loved them. The Chancellor had insisted it would be kinder not to encourage impossible hopes.
Mira looked at the row of lamps above the counter, each carrying a name in blue glass. One lamp had gone dark. Beside it, someone had set an empty saucer for a person who was expected to come home.
They walked toward the House of Returns through streets that changed names at every turn. Mira began to understand why the City of AsterVale had built such elaborate signs. If you could not leave a place, you made the place contain enough language that it could continue to feel like a world.
At a corner near the canal they passed a procession of children carrying paper birds. A girl looked directly at Cael. She wore a blue scarf and had a small white scar above one eyebrow. He stopped in the middle of the street.
Cael: Elian?
The girl looked at him as though at a picture from a book whose title she could not remember. Then she reached into her pocket and brought out a window she had drawn on a folded piece of paper. She had made it as a child to help her belongings feel less lonely. Mira recognized the detail from Cael's account. The girl was older than the one he remembered, perhaps sixteen. Time had continued for her even when memory had not.
Elian: I used to dream there was someone standing outside my room. He always had a red kite and told me it was really a bird because he couldn't make the wings stay straight.
Cael: It was a terrible kite.
Elian: You know it?
Cael: I made it.
She looked down at the folded paper. Her mouth trembled as she tried to fit a face to the shape of a memory. For a moment she seemed to be deciding whether to run away from the possibility or toward it. Then she stepped closer and asked him a question no one else in the city could have answered.
Elian: When we were frightened of the thunder, what did we call it?
Cael: A giant moving the furniture upstairs.
She laughed. It was the first sound Mira had heard from her that was not an effort to remember something. Elian held out her hand, and Cael took it. The scar on his wrist glowed faintly. The lamps above the market were still burning, but one had begun to shine brighter.
Mira turned away, not from embarrassment, but to give the reunion an ordinary space in which to happen. She was glad she had not promised him that his sister would return. She had promised only that they would find out. The difference was finally a kindness instead of a failure.
!sound bell
From every bell tower came a deep metallic tone. A printed notice appeared on the market board: THE AGREEMENT WILL CLOSE AT FIRST LIGHT. ALL RESIDENTS MUST REMAIN IN THEIR DESIGNATED DISTRICTS. The Chancellor's authority had found a way to cross the boundary without asking the people who lived behind it.
AsterVale's mayor stood before the fountain and read the notice aloud. The crowd listened in silence until a woman near the front asked whether the agreement required their consent. The mayor admitted that no copy of the original contract had ever been shown to the city. A man selling vegetables demanded to know why a contract he had never seen could govern his grandchildren. The murmuring grew into voices, then arguments, then the beginning of a public meeting.
Mira had spent years believing arguments were things people made when they had forgotten the value of evidence. Watching the market, she began to suspect that an argument could also be a kind of evidence: that a person cared enough about the future to speak where a command would have preferred silence.
? "Stand with Elian and hear the families before the assembly." -> families_heard
? "Go to the Council chamber and obtain the missing terms." -> chamber_heard
? "Walk the boundary to discover what the storm truly is." -> storm_heard

@scene families_heard
!set mercy += 2
!set bond += 1
!background hidden_market
!music memory
The House of Returns was full of desks arranged in circles instead of rows. Its teachers explained that children who had been separated from their families learned more readily when no one was made to sit with their back to another child. Each desk bore the name of a town beyond the boundary. Some names were no longer real places; others would become unreal within the hour.
Elian introduced Mira to the keeper of records, a quiet woman who had spent seventeen years writing down the last things visitors remembered before they forgot how to return. There were notebooks for parents, travelers, missing brothers, and people who had visited only once but left behind a small and irreplaceable kindness. No one had been permitted to mail these records. The House had been called sentimental for keeping them.
Mira: Sentiment is often what survives when institutions are finished explaining why they did nothing.
The keeper showed her a shelf of drawings made by children who had never seen their parents' faces. Every drawing was different. None was accurate. All of them were acts of witness. Mira added a line to her ledger: the children of AsterVale are not a rescue project; they are persons with the right to be wrong, to argue, and to decide whether the risks of the world belong to them too.
Cael and Elian sat together by the window. They spoke softly of an old river and a red kite. Mira could not hear the words. She did not need to. She had begun to trust that a world might contain joy even while it was breaking.
!jump night_before_vote

@scene chamber_heard
!set truth += 2
!background archive_fire
!music mystery
The Council chamber was circular and had no chair at its center. The architect had explained that no one should govern a country from a position where every other person had to look up. The Chancellor had subsequently arranged for her own seat to be built on a platform at the edge, where everyone still had to look up at her from almost every angle.
In the locked desk Mira found the amendments to the agreement. The original instrument had not been designed to erase cities. It had been designed to preserve names long enough for strangers to find one another across a storm. The Council had modified it when the first rescue required more power than the ordinary skies could provide. Every payment of a remembered person had increased the instrument's capacity to keep the cities separate.
The final amendment gave the Chancellor authority to choose the next payment without consulting the people affected. A list of proposed payments included the city of Bellwether and, beneath it, the name of the observatory itself.
Mira: The machine was built to remember. We changed it into an instrument for forgetting.
She took the amendments to the assembly. She would not spare the Council the humiliation of having its words read aloud in front of the people who had paid for them.
!jump night_before_vote

@scene storm_heard
!set wonder += 2
!background cloud_coast
!music storm
The boundary looked like a pale wall of rain. Beyond it the outer sky seemed at once very far away and immediately adjacent, as if distance had become a matter of permission rather than measurement. At the edge of the canal, Mira found an old telescope pointed not toward the stars but toward a region of darkness below the horizon.
She looked through it and saw the storm. It was not a cloud, and not a weather system. It was a pressure between histories: the friction of two worlds kept from occupying the same space. Wherever the lens moved, one version of a street appeared over another. A house could stand and fall in the same instant; a child could be born and never conceived, and both possibilities pulled against the glass with the force of an ocean.
Cael: If we open the passage, those worlds will meet.
Mira: Yes. But the storm isn't waiting outside the cities. The storm is what happens when we insist on keeping them apart.
The realization changed the question she would ask at the assembly. It was not whether the outer islands could survive a natural disaster. It was whether the instrument had become the disaster it was supposed to prevent.
!jump night_before_vote

@scene night_before_vote
!background hidden_market
!music memory
!show mira open at left
!show cael wary at right
By evening, the market had become a different place. Traders dismantled their stalls and carried the wooden frames to the assembly square to make benches. The candles that had been set out for a festival were moved under awnings to shelter the ballot tables from rain. The city had expected to celebrate the turn of the season. Instead it was preparing to decide whether the season itself might be permitted to change.
Mira and Cael walked beside the canal with Ilya, the printer whose newspaper had chronicled the city's losses. She wore ink on her cuffs and carried a bundle of proofs so large that she had tied it to her shoulders with a piece of sailcloth. She explained that the presses were running through the night. Every household would receive a notice with the questions the assembly intended to ask. The answers would not bind anyone outside the city, but Ilya wanted the record to show that the citizens had been capable of describing their own future before the Council attempted to describe it for them.
Ilya handed Mira a proof. The text was imperfect. Several paragraphs contradicted one another. A heading had been crossed out and replaced with a question. The last page contained a list of names whose holders had asked not to be counted with either faction. Mira had never seen an official document admit uncertainty so plainly.
Mira: Your notice looks unfinished.
Ilya: Of course it is. We're still alive.
They passed a theater where a company of actors was rehearsing a play for a performance that might never happen. The stage represented the city before the boundary. One actor carried a bucket of water while another stood at a window and described a harbor that none of the younger company had ever seen. The audience seats were empty except for a child eating an apple and an old man who appeared to be asleep. The actors continued without embarrassment. Mira asked why they were rehearsing on the night of the assembly.
The director said that if the city was restored, the play would become a history. If the city remained hidden, it would become a fantasy. Either way, the actors wanted to be able to tell it well. A person did not wait for history to choose the genre of a life before beginning to live it.
Near the old station they met a family packing a cart. The mother was frightened that the city's border would fail without warning and wanted to take her children to the safest district. Her partner argued that nowhere could be called safe while the machine decided where danger belonged. The eldest child had packed only a box of colored pencils. He said he planned to draw the sky the day the city opened, so he could remember whether the clouds had looked different from inside.
Mira helped the younger child tighten a strap on the cart. She wanted to offer reassurance. Instead she showed the parents a map of the passages she had seen and marked the routes that appeared stable. She told them what she did not know. The mother thanked her more warmly than Mira had expected. Perhaps honesty was not the opposite of comfort. Perhaps it was a form of respect that could remain when comfort failed.
!if mercy >= 3
A girl ran after them carrying a paper bag of bread. The baker had sent it, she explained, because the strangers had listened to the missing families earlier. The bread was still warm. Mira took one piece and gave the rest to the children by the cart. The girl's face brightened in recognition. For a few minutes, the world had the size of a kitchen and the shape of an ordinary evening.
!end
!if truth >= 4
At the station gate Mira found a notice nailed beneath the Council's old crest. It contained a paragraph cut from an early agreement and concealed from the public version: THE BENEFICIARIES SHALL NOT BE REQUIRED TO BE INFORMED OF THE SPECIFIC LOSSES BY WHICH CONTINUITY IS SECURED. The language made her hands cold. She copied it on the back of Ilya's proof so no official could later claim that ignorance had been an accident.
!end
!if wonder >= 4
The stars over the canal appeared in an unfamiliar configuration. Mira counted them twice. A constellation she had known since childhood had acquired a seventh point. She drew it in the margin of the assembly notice. It might be the sign of a newly restored memory, or a distortion caused by the failing boundary. For once she could let an unanswered question remain in the sky without feeling that she had failed it.
!end
Cael stopped in front of a row of houses with painted blue doors. A woman stood on a step mending a net. She glanced up as they approached, frowned, and then spoke to him as though he had forgotten an appointment. The familiarity struck him harder than anger would have. He answered carefully, unwilling to pretend to remember what he could not. She told him that her brother had sailed with Cael on the northern route and had returned once with a broken hand. Cael had sat through the night making a splint from a piece of broken furniture. The brother had laughed afterward and said that a man who could repair a hand from a chair could probably repair anything.
Cael remembered the chair. It had been painted green. He did not remember the brother's name. The woman supplied it and asked him to repeat it. He did, once and then again. She looked relieved in a way that suggested she had not expected him to know how to receive a gift without pretending it came from him.
The woman asked whether they would restore the city. Cael said he did not yet know what restoration should mean. She nodded, as if this were the first answer all day that sounded like a person speaking rather than an institution making a promise.
A clock began to strike. It was the same backward-running clock they had seen near the bakery. A boy on a ladder was adjusting its mechanism with a tool too large for his hand. Each strike sounded a little different, and every citizen in the street paused to count it, perhaps to decide whether it was now one hour earlier or one hour nearer the end. The boy called down that the clock would not be repaired until the assembly agreed on which direction it should run. Nobody corrected him.
Mira had grown up among people who believed time was a problem with a mechanical solution. She had never considered how much of time belonged to agreement: a birthday, an appointment, a closing hour, a promise to return. The clock could be made to run either way. The people would still have to decide when to meet.
Cael: I used to think this place was waiting for me to come back. Now I think it was living without me, and I was missing it.
Mira: That may be the hardest part of finding something lost. It has changed while you were trying to remember it.
Cael: Have you changed?
Mira looked at the ink on her fingers, the blue button in her pocket, and the small brass whistle whose owner she had never met. She thought of the woman in the red coat, the families on the coast, the print shop under the bridge, and the Council's signature on an experiment whose human consequences had been called an acceptable margin. She had left the observatory believing she needed to discover a fact. She was returning to it with a question about who had the right to decide what the fact meant.
Mira: I hope so.
They returned to the square as the first benches were set down. Ilya placed her printed questions on the tables and weighed the corners with small river stones. The mayor arrived carrying an empty box in which citizens could leave written objections. A woman who had once voted for the Council demanded that her name be included among those who supported a negotiated extension. A carpenter who feared the storm asked to speak against opening the passage immediately. Several younger people disagreed with both and refused to stand with either faction. No one had yet invented a method for reconciling all their wishes.
The machine would have preferred one answer. The square was full of people preparing to give many. Mira watched the lamps being lit one by one and felt something she had not expected: not confidence, not relief, but a kind of readiness to listen.
!jump the_beacon_watch

@scene the_beacon_watch
!background hidden_market
!music storm
!show mira troubled at left
!show cael wary at right
The assembly lamps had not yet been lit when the canal began to rise. At first the water moved only an inch, enough to make the floating candles brush the underside of the bridge. Then the current reversed. Barges that had been moored against the east bank pulled toward the west, their ropes tightening until the posts groaned. A fish seller shouted that the canal had done this once before, on the night the boundary closed. Someone ran to fetch the waterworks engineer. Someone else began carrying crates to higher ground. The market changed from an argument into a place where people had to keep each other alive.
Mira watched the surface for several seconds before recognizing the pattern. The water was not flooding from a river. It was moving toward the lens, drawn by the same pressure that had begun pulling the observatory out of its foundations. The city's walls were being asked to absorb a force they had never been designed to hold. The Council's agreement had kept the pressure hidden for so long that the people who lived beside the canal had come to think the water's strange habits were ordinary weather.
Ilya appeared with her arms full of printed notices. Her proofs were wet. She handed the driest copies to children, who carried them door to door and shouted directions to the high streets. The carpenter from the previous evening organized a line of people passing sandbags from the warehouse to the low bridge. A baker brought warm loaves and broke them into pieces because those working on the wall had no time to sit down. Nobody had elected a leader for the work. Several people began giving orders at once, and the orders were sometimes contradictory, but the wall rose because the workers could see the water and amend the instructions as it moved.
Mira: The pressure is going to find the weakest part of the boundary. We need to know where that is.
Cael: The eastern sluice. It was built for a much smaller current. If it fails, the hospital district will flood before anyone can move the patients.
Mira asked how he knew. He said he had sailed the canal before the boundary closed. The memory had returned with the smell of wet rope, incomplete but useful. She did not ask him to prove it. They ran toward the sluice with three volunteers and a coil of cable borrowed from the theater, where it had held a painted moon above a stage for twenty seasons.
The eastern gate was already bending. Water pressed against the iron plates, and blue light shone through the rivets. A woman in a dark green apron stood ankle-deep in the current with a wrench in one hand. She introduced herself as Adra, keeper of the pumps. Her assistant had gone to the hospital. She had been trying to release the emergency counterweight, but the controls were older than any engineer still alive. She had a manual. It was written in the same legal vocabulary that governed the lens. She had spent a lifetime repairing the city's water and had never been taught that the instructions for her oldest machine were a kind of contract.
Mira took the manual while Cael secured the cable to the gate. The emergency release required an operator to state the purpose of the waterworks and designate a permitted recipient of the redirected flow. The first field had been filled with a single word: CONTINUITY. The second was blank. The system would release water only if the operator agreed that the lives below the sluice could be used to preserve the structures above it. The blank was not an oversight. The blank was the place where an authority had expected to write the name of a district it was willing to sacrifice.
Adra: Can you make it open without filling that in?
Mira: I can refuse the old instruction. I don't know whether it will accept a new one.
Adra slammed her wrench against the iron until sparks rose. She said that if the machine refused to recognize a city whose people were drowning, then the machine could be made to open by force. Cael examined the bolts. He told her which would fail first and which were supporting the entire gate. The three of them began work. A city that was already tearing itself apart did not owe them the luxury of waiting for a philosophical solution.
The first bolt came free. Water struck the wall above it in a spray of blue droplets. Mira found the manual's page for emergency pressure relief and tore it out. She did not do this out of contempt for books. She did it because the page had been written so no one could change the instructions without destroying the document that made them binding. She fed the page into the current, and as it dissolved she spoke the words from memory, replacing the blank field with the names of the districts whose residents had come to help.
The counterweight moved. Not far; perhaps the length of a hand. But enough for Adra to wedge a bar beneath the plate. Cael swung his weight against the cable. On the third pull the gate opened and the water surged into an unused holding basin. The basin filled with a sound like a hundred doors slamming. The canal dropped by half a foot. Across the city, people on the sandbag line began to shout that the wall was holding.
A boy ran from the hospital with a message written on the back of a prescription. The patients had been moved upstairs. The water had reached the lower steps but gone no farther. The doctors needed blankets and clean drinking water. Adra sent her assistant to the public baths, where the reserve boilers could be brought into service. Ilya handed out the remaining dry copies of her notices and added a line in thick ink: THE EASTERN GATE WAS OPENED BY ITS KEEPERS AND THE PEOPLE WHO CAME TO HELP THEM.
Cael leaned against the wall, his hands bleeding where the cable had cut them. Mira took a strip of cloth from her sleeve and tied it around his palm. He protested that the wound was small. She said that smaller wounds were the ones people neglected until they became more troublesome than larger ones. He accepted the bandage without pretending it was unnecessary.
!if bond >= 2
Cael: You didn't ask the machine for permission.
Mira: I asked it a different question.
Cael: It seemed to dislike the question.
Mira: Then it has something in common with the Council.
He laughed, quiet and surprised. The sound made the ruined gate feel for one moment like a place where a future might begin. Neither spoke of what would happen to them when the last bell rang. They had been given a small interval of time, and they used it to sit beside each other and breathe.
!end
!if truth >= 4
Mira examined the manual more carefully after the crisis. The pages were numbered with the same cipher as the Council's hidden calculations. She copied the diagram of the gate and its release mechanism into her ledger. The instrument in the observatory would obey only certain forms of language, but the waterworks had just demonstrated that physical changes could create room for new language to be recognized. It was evidence that the final choice might not be limited to the Council's four permitted endings.
!end
The water receded. People began returning tools to their owners, although in the confusion nobody remembered who had brought some of them. The carpenter said they could keep the extra cable for the next flood. Adra said the sluice was no longer the same machine it had been that morning. It had broken a little and become more useful. Mira felt an unexpected affection for the bent iron gate. It would never qualify for the Council's definition of an elegant solution. It had saved the hospital district.
When the assembly bell sounded, the workers left the wall in shifts. Some went to the square still wearing wet clothes. Others stayed to watch the basin. A few declined to attend because their children needed supper. No one accused them of failing to participate in history. Mira crossed the bridge with Ilya's revised notice in her hand. It was smudged and poorly aligned, and it told the truth about something the people had done together before anyone had agreed on what should happen next.
!jump assembly

@scene assembly
!background hidden_market
!music threshold
!show mira open at left
!show cael resolved at right
The Emergency Assembly met beneath the open sky, because the Council chamber had become a place where people expected lies to be spoken in comfortable chairs. It was a disorderly meeting. There were questions from bakers, teachers, pilots, and children who did not understand why grown people had allowed a city to remain hidden without first deciding what would happen when it wanted to return. The mayor began with a prepared speech. A woman carrying a bucket interrupted to ask why the water was running backward. The mayor put away the speech.
The delegates had argued for an hour over whether the square was a suitable place to decide the fate of seventeen thousand people. The mayor wanted the chamber. The baker wanted a schoolhouse with enough chairs for every family. The clockmaker said there was no room big enough and that perhaps the city had spent too long believing important decisions required a special kind of room. In the end they carried benches out of shops and kitchens, arranged them in uneven circles, and left the central fountain uncovered so children could sit on its edge. No one could see everyone, but everyone could see someone.
Mira was asked to describe the machine. She tried to begin with the number of lenses, their alignments, and the mechanism by which a sentence could be given the force of a law of nature. A woman in a flour-dusted apron interrupted her before the second sentence. Would the bakery still have an oven tomorrow? Mira could not answer. Then a cart driver asked whether his daughters would remember their grandmother. She could not answer that either. A third question concerned the northern hospital. Was its stairwell likely to vanish if the boundaries changed? At last Mira put down her notes.
Mira: I can tell you what the machine is doing. I cannot tell you what it will do after we ask it to behave differently. My father claimed he could make that prediction. The city has spent seventeen years paying for his confidence.
Silence followed. It was not the silence of belief. It was the more demanding silence of people deciding what to do with an answer they did not like.
A surgeon from the eastern clinic stood. He had learned to operate in a city where the weather did not follow the season and children occasionally arrived at the hospital with memories older than their grandparents. He wanted the walls removed, he said, because medicine depended on exchange: the circulation of knowledge, medicine, and the experience of other people. Yet he had seen too many patients die because a confident authority had called a risk acceptable on their behalf. He would not let the Council make that calculation again. He did not want safety purchased with secrecy. He wanted danger described honestly, by people who would share it.
The baker asked what such honesty would look like at the end of a long night when frightened people wanted certainty. A young carpenter answered that perhaps it looked like this: a crowd trying not to interrupt one another, even when the sentence being spoken was unbearable. The baker laughed at him, then apologized, then asked him to continue. Their conversation took six minutes. The machine had never allowed six minutes for a citizen to change her mind.
Cael stood near the back, holding Ilya's engraving of the ship. Someone recognized the uniform on the deck and asked whether he had been on the last voyage through the boundary. He said yes. Another person accused him of having abandoned the city. Cael did not deny that he had left. He described the storm that carried him out, the injuries, the first weeks spent searching for a route home, and then the terrible slow loss of the knowledge that home existed. He could hear anger in the crowd; he let it remain anger. Mira had never seen him refuse the easier comfort of an explanation.
Cael: I could have searched harder while I still remembered. That much is mine to answer for. But what happened to the people you sent out afterward was not a failure of their affection. Someone made it impossible to come back and then called their absence a choice.
A woman in the front row covered her mouth. She had sent a son on a fishing boat ten years earlier. She asked whether Cael knew him. He did not. She asked whether that meant the boy was dead. He could not say. There was no answer large enough for the space between them. Cael came down from the step and sat beside her until the next speaker began.
Mira was beginning to understand that a public decision might not give anyone relief. It could give them something more difficult: a burden that was genuinely theirs, shared out under conditions they could inspect. The Council's bargain had promised to remove that burden forever. What it removed instead was the right to carry it.
Orsol appeared in the eastern lens, enormous as a mountain and as carefully composed as a portrait in a government building. She offered another generation of safety in exchange for the atlas and its evidence. The citizens could return to their work. The outer islands could continue to imagine themselves innocent of the agreement. The toll would be paid by places selected according to mathematical criteria. Nobody would need to know which places until after they were gone.
A child asked whether anyone would remember his birthday. Orsol said the question was sentimental. A hundred people began to speak at once.
Mira had once believed that the opposite of secrecy was disclosure. In the market she learned that disclosure was only the beginning. Facts placed in the hands of people who could not act on them were another form of decoration. The people needed time, language, and the knowledge that their votes could change something. They had been denied all three.
Mayor: We have seventeen thousand residents. The time remaining would not allow a ballot taken from every household. We can send three delegates, elected here and witnessed by the outer world, to speak for the city. But their decision must be limited. No one has the right to make an irreversible bargain that the living cannot revise.
Mira: That is exactly the principle the Council refused to put in the contract.
Orsol: A city cannot vote to undo the laws of physics.
Mira: It can vote to stop calling your decision a law of physics.
The Chancellor's face tightened. Mira realized how rarely anyone had addressed her without first asking permission to speak. Orsol looked older than she had when Mira was a child, and yet the same in the precise way a clock looks the same after years of measuring other people's lives.
Elian stepped onto the fountain. She was young, and the crowd fell quiet not because youth gave her authority but because the people understood she had lived the consequence of a decision made before she could consent.
Elian: When I was little, I was told that my brother had chosen to leave. Later I was told I had imagined him. For years I was ashamed of remembering somebody who supposedly didn't exist. I have lived here all my life and I love this city. I don't want to lose it. But if keeping it means teaching another girl that her brother never loved her, I don't want that bargain to continue in my name.
Cael stood below the fountain with both hands closed at his sides. Mira saw him trying not to make the moment about his grief. She admired him for it more than she could have explained. Elian had spoken for herself. He had not interrupted to provide a better version of her story.
A teacher spoke about the schools and the libraries that would be lost if the cities collapsed back into the outside world. A gardener spoke about the children who had been born inside the boundary, who did not remember any other sky. A pilot argued that the storm might be navigable if it could be studied rather than feared. A mother asked why the choice had to be between an everlasting prison and a catastrophe that killed everyone.
That was the question the machine did not know how to answer. Its terms were binary: keep the world divided or pay the debt by reunifying it. The instrument had no provision for a third way. The Council had built a government around that limitation, and for seventeen years no one had been permitted to question whether the limitation belonged to the universe or merely to the instrument.
Mira remembered her father's letter. He had written that he had failed to make the erasure gentle. He had not written that he failed to make it reversible. The distinction felt intentional. A person who knew how to build a device sometimes left the most important part of the instructions in the form of an apology.
She asked the delegates to wait while she returned to the eastern lens. The mayor objected that they had little time; Orsol objected that time had already run out. Mira answered that neither of them would know until someone asked the machine. Cael volunteered to accompany her.
The delegates were chosen. There was a representative of the families, a representative of the occupations that kept the city alive, and a child chosen by the other children to speak about the future they expected to inhabit. Mira was relieved they had not chosen her. She had come to give the hidden city a voice, not to acquire one more place where hers counted most.
!sound bell
The sky above the market bent into a pale ring. The atlas began pulling at its brass hinges, as if a great tide had formed between its pages. The closing had begun earlier than predicted. Orsol's projection faded, and in the silence that followed Mira could hear the frightened breathing of the people around her.
Cael: We have to go now.
Mira: Then we go with what they've told us. No other story will be accepted as their answer.
They returned through the lens carrying the elected delegation's instructions and the knowledge that the people of AsterVale had already begun deciding what to do with a future that had been withheld from them. Mira kept thinking of the child's question about birthdays. The sky was full of grand instruments. None of them could tell her whether anyone would remember the next birthday.
!jump the_people_at_the_gate

@scene the_people_at_the_gate
!background lost_city
!music threshold
!show mira open at left
!show cael resolved at right
After the assembly, the delegates did not immediately follow Mira to the lens. They had households to inform, tools to collect, and arguments still being made in the streets. The people had agreed on the right to decide; they had not agreed on every consequence of their decision. The mayor asked whether they could take another hour. Mira could see the pale ring over the city closing by degrees and knew the machine was unlikely to grant one. She said they could take the time needed to make the instructions truthful, but that no delay would be free of risk. The mayor thanked her for not calling the risk imaginary.
Three delegates had been chosen, and each came to the gate carrying something different. The families' representative carried a book of names with blank lines left for people who had not yet been found. The representative of the city's trades brought a list of food stores, medicines, ships, and working machinery, because a city returned to the world would need more than the right to exist. The child chosen by the children carried a small tin box of drawings. One showed the observatory as a house built on clouds. Another showed AsterVale as a circle surrounded by doors. The last contained no picture at all, only a sentence written with great care: PLEASE DO NOT MAKE US PROMISE TO BE BRAVE.
Mira read it twice. She thought of Tessa from the weather station, of children in the hospital, of the boy who had tried to make the backward clock run again. Adults were fond of demanding courage from children after making the conditions in which courage became necessary. She folded the drawing and put it back in its box. The child watched until she had closed the lid.
At the boundary, a line of Council officials waited under a canopy of blue silk. Their leader was a man Mira had seen at public lectures, where he introduced the Chancellor with speeches about responsible governance. He carried an order bearing Orsol's seal. By authority of the first agreement, he declared, no citizen was permitted to leave the hidden city while the system remained unstable. The order used the same words with which the Council had once described the original confinement as voluntary.
The families' delegate stepped forward. She asked whether the official had received permission from the people who lived in AsterVale. He said that the agreement had been made before the birth of most current residents. She asked whether the people who had been born under it were permitted to reject it. He replied that such matters were beyond his jurisdiction. Then, with the expression of a man who believed he had said something decisive, he asked them to return to their homes.
Cael: You have come to a place where the people are arguing about their own future. You may join the argument. You cannot end it by pointing at a paper no one here was allowed to read.
The official looked at Cael's scar and paled. He recognized a mark he had never expected to see outside a Council archive. He demanded that Cael submit to an inspection. Cael held out his hand, not in surrender but to make the mark visible to everyone. Several people in the crowd stepped closer. They wanted to know what it meant that a courier had been made part of the machinery of their captivity. The official's answer was a long explanation about custodial authority. Someone laughed. The laughter spread, not because the subject was amusing, but because the language was so far removed from the lives it claimed to govern.
The official ordered his guards to block the passage. They hesitated. One guard had grown up in the hidden city and recognized the woman standing beside the families' delegate as his aunt. Another saw the hospital workers carrying blankets through the crowd and stepped out of their way. The order had been written for men who would act like pieces on a board. The men were human beings standing in a street where other human beings knew their names.
Mira took the old agreement from her satchel. She did not tear it up. She read its most important clause aloud, including the portion the Council had never published, the clause that said the people benefiting from the bargain need not be informed of the losses. She let the words settle over the crowd. The official's face remained composed, but he had to lower his eyes to the seal in his hand. The seal was still valid under the rules of the machine. It was no longer convincing to the people standing in front of him.
Mira: You may take this paper back to the Chancellor. Tell her the city has read it. Tell her the people whose names it did not include have something to add.
The guard who had recognized his aunt moved aside. Others followed. The official said they were disobeying a lawful order. The aunt said she had a lawful duty to return a borrowed kettle and that the guard could come to supper afterward. The absurdity broke the remaining tension. The passage cleared a little at a time, not because someone had defeated the Council but because enough people refused to make the Council's authority the only thing in the street.
Elian appeared beside Cael. She had brought a piece of the blue scarf from the bakery, the one that held a memory of him returning for her when they were children. She asked him whether he would remember her after he crossed the lens again. He told her that he did not know. Then he took the scarf and tied it around his wrist above the compass scar.
Cael: If I forget, this will be the beginning of the story when you tell it to me again.
Elian: And if the story changes?
Cael: Then you can tell me what I got wrong. I would like to be corrected by someone who was there.
She touched his bandaged hand. For a moment Mira saw the two siblings not as symbols of the passage's cruelty but as people recovering the ordinary awkwardness of having grown older without each other. Elian asked whether Cael still hated pears. He said he had never liked them. She laughed and told him he had once eaten six in a row to win a bet. He could not remember the wager. He accepted the humiliation with more grace than he had accepted the Council's seal.
The delegates stepped into the passage. The city moved behind them: lanterns being put away, water being pumped from the lower streets, children arguing about whether the sky on the other side would contain the same stars. Not everyone followed. Many stayed to tend the places they had built. Mira understood that their decision to remain was as important as the delegates' decision to leave. The right to return had to include the right not to be moved against one's will.
The passage trembled. AsterVale's lamps blurred against the blue glass. For one dreadful instant the families' book became transparent, and the written names shone through its pages like fish beneath ice. Mira pressed her hand against the cover and spoke the first name aloud. Cael spoke the second. The trades' delegate spoke the third. Other people took up the recitation until the book grew solid again. The child with the tin box listened, then began saying the names of her classmates. Some of the names were repeated. Nobody objected.
They reached the observatory side carrying imperfect instructions, frightened people, and a box of drawings that a machine had never been built to understand. The assembly was over. The work it had begun was not.
!jump return_to_lens

@scene return_to_lens
!background observatory_storm
!music storm
!show mira troubled at left
!show cael wary at right
The observatory was no longer stable. Its floors had developed a slow inclination toward the eastern lens, as if the building had begun sliding down a slope no architect had calculated. Doors opened on rooms they did not lead to. A staircase from the music hall appeared in the star library; the maintenance corridor ended at a window through which Mira could see her childhood kitchen. Her mother was putting salt on the sill. The scene lasted one heartbeat. Then the window looked out on lightning again.
Before entering the control chamber, Mira returned to the little room where she had slept as a child. Its door had reappeared between two stone columns as the building shifted. The room was smaller than she remembered. This was almost a relief; memory should have its ordinary embarrassments as well as its terrors. The bed was still beneath the window, and a paper constellation hung from the ceiling on a thread of blue silk. Her mother had cut the stars from the backs of rejected Council documents. The shapes were imperfect. One had six points and another nine. Mira had been very young when her mother explained that the number of points did not matter if the child who named a star knew how to find it again.
Cael stopped at the doorway. The shifting architecture seemed to have made him cautious of crossing any threshold without an invitation. Mira remembered how he had stood outside the west door on the night of his arrival, offering a letter from a vanished city without demanding that she accept it. The gesture had seemed almost theatrical then. Now it was the beginning of a pattern. He did not want to become one more person who made decisions for her while pretending the decisions were gifts.
Mira: I used to hide here when the Council held its dinners. They would bring guests upstairs to look at the telescopes and speak about the world as though the world were a property they administered. My father used to bring me a plate of cakes and tell me to stay quiet until the speeches were over.
Cael: You must have been an unusually obedient child.
Mira: I was excellent at staying quiet while making elaborate plans to ruin something later.
She lifted the paper constellation. Written on the back of the largest star were three words in her mother's handwriting: LET HER CHOOSE. The instruction had no obvious subject except Mira herself. She thought of her father, who had tried to keep the world safe by denying it choices, and her mother, who had left a child's toy with an unfinished sentence about freedom. Love could be protective. It could also be afraid. A choice was not a promise that nothing would hurt. It was an agreement not to borrow another person's life without asking.
She tucked the star inside the atlas. It left a pale outline on the blank page, as though the paper were considering where to put it. Cael watched the shape appear but said nothing. They stood together long enough for the room to remember the people who had lived in it. Then the door began to vanish behind them, and they returned to the control chamber by the corridor that still existed.
Cael carried the atlas under one arm and the delegation's sealed account under the other. The paper had grown heavy. Every word it contained represented something the machine's old agreement did not know how to hold: a right to decide, a right to revise, a right to change one's mind even after a safe choice had become a dangerous tradition.
Mira took the charred contract if she had it, or the names, or the witness's clapper, and placed it beside the atlas. The three records had not become more complete during the journey. But the missing parts now had witnesses in the world. That mattered more than the machine expected.
She examined the base of the eastern lens. Behind a panel bearing her father's name she found a second mechanism: an unfinished ring of twelve slots, one empty, eleven filled with little glass bottles. In each bottle floated a word. She could read some of them. Mercy. Silence. Debt. Arrival. Home. The final bottle, smallest and nearest the empty slot, contained the word Again.
Mira: It's a grammar.
Cael: I beg your pardon?
Mira: The instrument isn't just a device. It constructs a statement about the world. We are living in the conclusion of a sentence written seventeen years ago. The missing slot is where the sentence can be changed.
She opened her father's maintenance notes. Between diagrams of gears were fragments of an abandoned language in which a command became binding only when spoken by two parties who understood the same word differently. Her father had tried to design an opening clause that would allow the agreement to be renewed by those who were excluded. The Chancellor had forbidden him to complete it.
Cael: What would filling the slot do?
Mira: Give the city the chance to write a new clause. But the machine has to recognize the voices. It recognizes my father's blood, your scar, and the delegates' authority. It might accept us as witnesses if we can bring them to the lens.
Cael: The delegates are at the market. We can fetch them.
Mira: And Orsol will be at the eastern stair when the bell strikes. If she reaches the control ring first, she can lock the clause again. We may not have time to do both.
Cael looked toward the door. The stonework of the observatory shook. A coil of copper fell from the ceiling. He caught it before it struck the atlas, then stared at the metal in his hands as if it had reminded him of something he had been unable to say.
Cael: I know this ring. I was here when it was built. I wasn't a child. I was the messenger your father called to carry the first words through the lens. We thought we were saving the cities. When the toll began taking names, I tried to tell them. They made me forget so that I would keep returning with another message.
Mira: You remember now?
Cael: Pieces. I remember carrying a letter addressed to you when you were seven. I remember Elian crying because I could not stay. I remember your father burning the compass into my wrist. He apologized while he did it. I told him the apology would not matter unless he stopped.
The room grew quiet around them. Mira had often imagined her father as a victim of the Council. Now she saw that his grief had come with responsibilities. He had tried to repair the harm. He had also caused it. Love and culpability were not opposites; a person could carry both, and the people who loved him could be asked to carry the knowledge without being asked to excuse it.
She looked into the lens. Her mother's remembered figure stood far away in the darkness, not moving. Mira wanted to ask whether her father had ever found a way to forgive himself. She did not. The question would have been another attempt to make the dead answer for the living.
? "Send Cael for the delegates; confront Orsol yourself." -> face_orsol
? "Go together to the market and bring everyone back." -> fetch_delegates
? "Try the unfinished grammar now with the witnesses you have." -> write_clause

@scene face_orsol
!set truth += 2
!set bond += 1
!set promise = "confront"
!background lens_corridor
!music threshold
Cael hesitated at the doorway. Mira asked him to trust the people of AsterVale to travel without the Council's permission and to return with the delegates. He nodded, but before leaving he placed his old watch in her hand. Its face was dark blue and had no hands. He had carried it for years because a person without a reliable history sometimes needed a reminder that time continued even when the records did not.
Cael: Give it back when we meet on the other side.
Mira: And if we don't?
Cael: Then it can remind you that promises are not guarantees.
Mira went to the eastern stair. Orsol was waiting beneath the portraits of the first directors, carrying the official seal that could close the lens. The Chancellor did not look surprised to see her alone. She had expected Mira to choose confrontation, perhaps because Mira had spent most of her adolescence perfecting the art of arguing with people who held power over her.
Orsol: Do you have the atlas?
Mira: Yes. I also have the voices of the people you tried to make silent.
Orsol: The people have no idea what they are choosing.
Mira: They know they were not asked before. They understand that better than you do.
The Chancellor held out the seal. For the first time Mira saw that Orsol's hand was shaking. Her authority had concealed fear so long that she had almost succeeded in making it a private virtue. Mira wondered whether the Chancellor had once been a decent person who failed to bear the sight of a disaster and then spent the rest of her life making sure no one could contradict the decision she had made in its shadow.
Orsol: If I am wrong, everything I have done was cruelty.
Mira: You don't need me to make you innocent. You need to decide what you will do now.
The seal lost its blue light. Orsol lowered it, and for one moment she looked like a tired woman standing in an empty corridor while a building she loved came apart around her.
!jump the_last_unwritten_clause

@scene fetch_delegates
!set mercy += 2
!set bond += 2
!set promise = "delegates"
!background hidden_market
!music journey
Mira and Cael ran through the passage together. The market was almost empty. Its stalls had been closed, the lanterns dimmed, and the elected delegates were waiting beside the fountain with a notebook of instructions. There was no heroic procession, only people carrying what they might need for a difficult journey: warm coats, dry bread, medicine, and letters intended for children who might not be there when they returned.
Elian stood beside the delegates. She had found a photograph of Cael in the House of Returns, taken years before the agreement. In it he was smiling at someone outside the frame. She had no memory of the afternoon, but she had decided the photograph was not evidence that she had imagined him. It was evidence that they had both existed before the Council told them how to remember.
Elian: Take me with you.
Cael: The journey isn't safe.
Elian: You came for me without asking whether it was safe. I can come for you on the same terms.
Mira understood that Cael wanted to refuse because he loved his sister. She also understood that Elian had spent years having other people refuse choices on her behalf. She gave Cael enough time to look at the floor before she told Elian the risks honestly.
The group crossed back to the observatory. The floor shook beneath their feet. In the east wing, Orsol's guards had formed a line across the staircase. The delegates asked whether the guards had been elected to represent the city. None could answer. The line broke, not because the guards were frightened, but because their orders contained no language for a question that was impossible to evade.
!jump the_last_unwritten_clause

@scene write_clause
!set wonder += 2
!set truth += 1
!set promise = "grammar"
!background undersea_lens
!music mystery
Mira placed her palm on the empty bottle and spoke the word that her father had left unspoken: Again. The ring answered with a vibration so deep it seemed to arise from the bones of the observatory. The eleven filled bottles began to exchange their words, forming combinations Mira could almost read. The lens displayed the cities as overlapping diagrams, their streets aligned at the places where people on either side still remembered one another.
Cael touched the compass scar. His voice entered the machine not as a command but as a witness. He described the red kite, the backward clock, the bakery where he had first learned to make a poor joke at the right time. The lens recorded his memories without erasing them. Mira realized the instrument could hold two histories at once when neither insisted on making the other disappear.
Mira: We were taught that the machine can keep or release the cities. What if its real function is to remember the boundary without forcing us to live inside it?
The silver ring flickered. For an instant she saw a third version of the sky, one in which the hidden cities remained visible and connected to the outer world by bridges of light. Then the vision collapsed. The machine required the voices of the city to make it stable. The delegates would still have to choose whether to complete the clause.
!jump the_last_unwritten_clause

@scene the_last_unwritten_clause
!background shattered_lens
!music storm
!show mira luminous at left
!show cael resolved at right
The eastern lens had grown so large that the machinery around it looked like scaffolding built by children. Its brass rings rotated in opposite directions, and between them the air was full of torn instructions. Mira could read fragments as they passed: CONTINUITY, NECESSITY, ACCEPTABLE LOSS, PRESERVATION OF THE WHOLE. The words were not lies in themselves. They became lies when the machine treated them as complete sentences and refused to name the people on whom their completion depended.
The Council's Chancellor stood on the far platform. Orsol had removed the ceremonial coat she wore for public appearances. Beneath it she looked older and more tired than the image projected over AsterVale's market. Her sleeves were rolled to the elbows. A thin line of blood crossed one wrist where the control ring had cut her. She was still holding the official seal, though its blue light had dimmed until it resembled a dying ember.
Orsol: I hoped you would come to negotiate. I see you have brought witnesses.
Mira: I brought people. They came because the decision concerns them.
Orsol: People are poor instruments for determining what must be done in a crisis.
Mira: Then perhaps the crisis should not have been designed around the needs of an instrument.
Orsol did not answer immediately. She looked past Mira to Cael's scarf and to the delegation standing behind them. The child held her box of drawings against her chest. Orsol appeared to notice it, and for a moment her expression changed into something like recognition. She asked the child why she had come. The child said that the adults had already made too many decisions without asking what children wished to remember. Orsol said memory could be dangerous. The child replied that forgetting had not made any of them safe.
The lens responded to the exchange with a low vibration. It had recognized the voices but not their authority. Its grammar was old and precise. To change a world, it wanted to know who was authorized to speak for that world. The first agreement had given an answer that no living citizen could challenge. Mira's father's unfinished ring had been an attempt to make that answer revisable. The last empty slot waited beside the control table, shaped like a space where an object should have been laid but never was.
Mira opened her ledger. She put the names of the missing on one side and the Council's calculations on the other. The pages lifted in the wind. She spoke a sentence describing the harm done by the original agreement. The machine answered by showing the disasters that the agreement had prevented: storms held at the boundary, floods diverted from cities, plagues stopped before they spread. The images were real enough to frighten her. The Council had not invented the danger. It had used the danger to claim a permission nobody had granted.
Cael: We are not saying the danger was imaginary.
Orsol: Then what will you do with the people who die if you remove the wall?
Cael: We will remember them. And we will tell the living what we know before we ask them to bear the risk.
Orsol shook her head. She said remembrance was not a defense against suffering. Cael agreed. The simple answer seemed to strike her harder than an argument. She had been trained to meet accusations of cruelty with an account of necessity. She had not prepared for people who could acknowledge necessity and still refuse the secrecy with which she had wrapped it.
The control platform shook. One of the brass rings cracked along a line of old repairs. Mira saw the dates stamped into the metal: the first test at the ferry landing, the vanished school, the garden, the northern catastrophe, Bellwether, AsterVale. The system had been patched repeatedly without anyone returning to ask whether its first premise remained defensible. Every repair had made the machinery larger. Every repair had made the people affected by it more distant from the people authorized to operate it.
She thought of the water gate in the city. Adra had not waited for the old instructions to become just. She had turned the bolts and found a way to keep the hospital standing. The lesson was not that machines could always be made harmless. It was that a person who understood a machine could refuse to let the machine's existing rules exhaust the possibilities for action.
Mira: The empty slot is not meant for a new ruler. It is meant for a condition.
Orsol: Which condition?
Mira: That no agreement can be made permanent merely by making its victims unable to remember that it exists.
The words hung in the air. The lens attempted to parse them. It showed an error: CONDITION REQUIRES AN AUTHORITY. Mira turned to the delegates. She asked them to speak the names of the people who had instructed them, not as a spell but as a record of whom they could answer to afterward. The families' representative began with the missing. The trades' representative named the workers who would have to repair the city. The child named the children who had asked her to carry their drawings. Cael added his own name and Elian's. Mira spoke her father's name, then her mother's, then her own.
The ring moved. One of the old instructions fell away like a flake of burnt paper. Across the glass appeared the outline of a doorway whose frame was made from the names being spoken. Beyond it, Mira could see a shore under an ordinary sky. The machine had found a way to represent a choice made by people rather than one imposed upon them. It had not yet found a way to ensure the choice would end safely.
The Chancellor approached. She said that if Mira opened the passage, she would be responsible for the suffering that followed. Mira answered that she would be responsible for the part she chose, including the decision to leave others free to make choices of their own. She would not accept responsibility for every disaster the Council had hidden beneath the word safety. Neither would she claim innocence merely because she had acted with good intentions.
Orsol looked at the control ring. Her hand was trembling. She had spent the better part of a lifetime believing the world would end if the agreement were changed. The possibility that the world could survive her loss of authority seemed to frighten her almost as much as the storms. She did not hand over the seal. She did not attempt to stop Mira from approaching the empty slot. For the first time that evening, she allowed the instrument to wait while someone else spoke.
Mira could feel the choices gathering behind the lens. They were not four easy futures. Each would ask something different of the people who had survived the long concealment. One would open the sky to danger and reunite the cities. One would require a living witness to keep the passage from closing. One would make a public bargain that could be revised. One would tear up the agreement and leave the world to govern its own broken mechanisms. The machine could show consequences. It could not decide which consequences a free people ought to choose.
The child with the drawings stepped beside Mira. She placed her tin box on the table and opened it to the picture of AsterVale surrounded by doors. Beneath the drawing she had written another sentence, smaller than the first: SOME DOORS SHOULD BE HARD TO CLOSE. Mira took the picture and laid it in the empty slot. The brass ring brightened. A sentence appeared across the lens in letters large enough for the people on both sides to read: THE AGREEMENT MAY BE CHANGED BY THOSE WHO MUST LIVE UNDER IT.
The floor stopped moving for one heartbeat. Mira knew the pause would not last. The old machinery was still failing, the storms still gathering, and the people on both sides still afraid. But the machine had recognized a clause it had never recognized before. There was now a space in its language where a choice could be made without first erasing the person who would have to live with it.
!jump final_engine

@scene final_engine
!background shattered_lens
!music storm
!show mira luminous at left
!show cael resolved at right
The last bell began to ring. It did not stop. Its sound moved through the observatory as a physical force, breaking the remaining windows and lifting pages into the air. The atlas rose from the table and opened to the blank place where Bellwether had been. Each vanished name appeared for one instant in a halo of blue light, then fell backward into the pages like rain.
The machine's twelve rings bore the marks of all the hands that had turned them. Here was a dent from her father's instrument, there a gouge where a previous director had tried to force a locked position, and lower down a narrow polished stripe at the height of a child. Mira put her palm against that stripe and recognized her own scale of motion. When she was seven, she had believed that helping her father turn a wheel meant helping him turn the stars. He had allowed the misunderstanding. Later he had allowed much worse ones.
The air beyond the lens became transparent. She saw the upper islands, the steeples of Bellwether, the markets of AsterVale, the little school with maps of impossible bedrooms. She saw a hospital at the coast where people waited beneath a leaking roof. She saw a grandmother sewing her name into a coat she hoped would outlast her. The machine had reduced all these things to a system of values. It had numbers for loss, continuity, risk, and projected suffering. Mira had learned to admire numbers because they could reveal what a human eye would miss. She still admired them. But numbers did not tell her who had been allowed to define what counted as suffering, or what another person might willingly risk to keep the life that belonged to them.
Cael took his repaired watch from his pocket. It still had no hands. He set it on the control ring where the Council's seal would once have rested. Its empty face showed the reflection of Mira's own. Neither of them could measure how much time remained. The bell made measurement impossible, each stroke arriving before the previous one had faded. She thought of the dockmaster's register, the printer's proofs, the child's mechanical bird, the doctor's refusal to call an unknown danger acceptable on somebody else's behalf. None of those witnesses had offered a perfect plan. Together they had made a different kind of map.
Mira: I thought my task was to restore the missing places. Then I thought it was to punish the people who took them. Now I think both were too small. A place cannot belong to a ledger, even a truthful one. It belongs to the people who have to wake up there tomorrow.
Cael: And if there is no tomorrow?
Mira: Then they should at least have been asked what they wished to do with tonight.
The eastern lens expanded until it filled the room. AsterVale became visible behind it, enormous and impossibly near. Mira could see the market square, the House of Returns, the backward clock, and Elian's blue scarf moving through a crowd. The city was no longer something she could place on a map. It was a place full of people asking to be allowed to remain real.
Orsol stood beside the main control ring. She had the official seal in one hand, but its light had faded. The delegates, or their written instructions, had reached the observatory. Their words appeared around the lens, not as a single decision but as several demands: the right to remain, the right to leave, the right to remember, the right to refuse a rescue that was indistinguishable from imprisonment.
Cael: It's listening.
Mira: No. It is waiting for someone to give it a command. We need it to understand that listening is different.
On the far side of the glass stood the Keeper of the Threshold. It wore the same small silver coat, and its face was older than any face should be allowed to become while still resembling a child. It pointed to the empty slot in the twelvefold ring.
Keeper: The first agreement was a statement about what must be lost. The new one must be a statement about what can be shared. But every statement has a cost. Someone will have to teach the machine a word it has never understood.
Mira looked down at her father's watch in Cael's hand, at the ledger of names, at the page of the contract or the clapper of the witness's bell. None was a perfect tool. All had become useful because someone had refused to allow them to be the last word.
The lens displayed four outcomes. In the first, the cities rejoined the world and the storm was allowed to pass through them, though not without danger. In the second, a living witness entered the instrument and became the memory that held the passage open. In the third, the Council's agreement was renewed for one final generation under public supervision, preserving the world as it was while the citizens prepared for a safer return. In the fourth, the instrument was destroyed; no bargain could be made again, and the world would have to bear both the missing places and the responsibility for finding them by ordinary means.
Cael: Which of those is the right choice?
Mira: I don't know. I used to think the purpose of studying the stars was to learn enough that I would never have to say that. Now I think the stars have been trying to teach me how to say it properly.
Orsol raised her face toward the lens. The Chancellor had spent her life believing that a person in authority was obliged to be certain. She looked at the four possibilities and, for the first time, did not announce that one of them was necessary.
Orsol: The city has given us its conditions. What do you propose to write into the final clause?
Mira looked at Cael. He looked back without attempting to choose for her. The atlas was open, its blank places waiting. Beyond the windows the first light of an unfinished morning touched the clouds.
Mira understood then that an ending was not a place where a story ceased. It was a place where the living accepted that the consequences of their choices would outlast the sentences in which they described them.
? "Open the sky and accept the danger together." -> ending_open
? "Become the living witness and keep the passage open." -> ending_witness
? "Negotiate a public, temporary covenant." -> ending_covenant
? "Destroy the bargain and entrust the world to its people." -> ending_unbound

@scene ending_open
!background first_morning
!music dawn
!panel parchment
!show mira luminous at left
!show cael resolved at right
Mira spoke the new clause without raising her voice: The people who live in the places held apart shall have the right to return, and those who live outside shall bear the passage with them. No city shall be removed from the world to spare another city the knowledge of danger. The machine shall open what it has closed, and the people shall learn to live with the weather it creates.
The atlas began to tremble. Every line on its surface brightened until the book seemed to contain a sunrise inside its pages. Across the hidden city the bells rang. The delegates repeated the clause in their own words, each insisting on one small correction: that those who chose to remain inside the boundary could do so, that children would be told why their families were separated, that names would be restored before buildings, because buildings could be rebuilt and names required someone to speak them.
Orsol listened. She did not agree to every correction. This time no one required her agreement. The final decision had been made where its consequences would be lived.
!sound bell
The lens opened. It was not the clean opening of a door; it was the tearing of a seam that had held two cloths together for years. Clouds spilled through streets. Bridges that had never touched one another found their ends, groaning as stone met stone. AsterVale emerged above the ocean of clouds and the outer world appeared around it like a long-forgotten shore returning with the tide.
The storm came with the reunification. It was a storm of things trying to be in more than one place at once: two roads crossing where only one could fit, two summers arguing over the date, two songs remembered by different people with different endings. Mira understood that the machine had not exaggerated the danger. There were houses whose walls fractured under the pressure of competing histories. There were people who vanished for a moment and returned in different clothing, carrying memories of days they had never lived.
Cael: The market is moving!
Mira: It is finding the place where it belongs. Help the people out of the streets.
They ran. The new bridges were narrow and unstable. Mira used the atlas as a guide, not a weapon, tracing where the edges of the histories could meet without breaking. Cael carried messages between the islands, his old courier skill returning as naturally as breathing. At every crossing he shouted the same instruction: speak the name of the person beside you. Remember whose hand you are holding.
The method was not magic. It was the opposite of the magic that had caused the disaster. When people spoke one another's names, the atlas found places for their histories to rest. It could not prevent every loss. It could make the losses less solitary.
!if bond >= 2
Cael found Mira on a collapsing stairwell. He took her hand without saying anything, and she recognized the gesture from the reflection in the glass passage: a future in which one person had reached for the other before either could fall. She had feared that the reflection was a command. Now she understood it had only been a possibility. They had chosen to make it true.
!end
!if bond < 2
Cael crossed the broken stairwell with a rope and held it steady while Mira climbed. They had not become easy with one another, but each had learned to trust the other's competence where words still failed. It was not the same as friendship. It was the groundwork on which friendship could be built.
!end
The storm lasted until morning. By then the map had altered beyond recognition. The northern coast had acquired three new bays, a floating island had settled beside Bellwether, and the observatory had lost half its western wing. In the eastern tower the great lens remained as a clear window, no longer a gate that demanded payment.
Mira went down to the market where Elian was helping a frightened child find her parents. The girl wore the blue scarf. There was flour on her sleeves, and she had the solemn expression of someone discovering that the work of an ordinary day can become heroic without asking permission.
Elian: It's terrible. Some of the streets are gone, and the river runs into the sky. But yesterday I didn't know whether there would ever be a tomorrow that belonged to me. Today I have too much tomorrow to know where to put it.
Mira: We can start by repairing the bridges.
They did. In the following months people argued about borders, taxation, where the new weather stations should stand, and whether it was fair for the citizens of AsterVale to keep their old property after the city had been restored. There were protests, elections, songs too sentimental to be respectable, and a great deal of paperwork. Mira found that she loved the paperwork more than she had expected. It was evidence that the people were making their own future badly, noisily, and together.
The Chancellor resigned. She testified before a public commission and did not claim the necessity of her actions as an excuse. Some people forgave her. Others never would. Her name remained in the records. Mira believed that was a more difficult form of justice than erasing it.
If the contract had survived, it became the first exhibit in the new Museum of Held Histories. If the names had survived, they were read from the highest bridge each year on the anniversary of the opening. If the witness had survived, the Keeper took a room at the House of Returns and began to learn what a child was allowed to want.
!if truth >= 5
Mira published the hidden amendments alongside her father's confession. The book was neither a defense nor a prosecution. It was an account of people who had made a terrible decision, people who had profited from it, and people who had been made to pay for it without being told. She was criticized for refusing to give the story a single villain. She answered that the absence of a simple villain was never evidence of the absence of harm.
!end
!if mercy >= 5
The woman in the red coat came to the observatory carrying a small embroidered star. Her daughter stood beside her, older than Mira had imagined, with the patient embarrassment of a person whose mother had insisted on telling every stranger how extraordinary it was to have her home. They thanked Mira. Mira told them that they owed her nothing. The woman replied that gratitude was not a debt, and Mira was pleased to learn a distinction she had not thought to make.
!end
One evening, nearly a year after the opening, Mira and Cael sat on the balcony above the clouds. The new maps were unfinished. New islands appeared whenever someone found an old name for a place the machine had hidden. AsterVale had become a city that every cartographer wished to visit and no cartographer could agree how to represent.
Cael: I used to think a map was a promise that nothing would change before I got there.
Mira: And now?
Cael: Now I think it is an invitation to discover how much has changed.
Mira looked toward Bellwether. She could remember the blue door of the bakery. She could remember her father's face. His voice remained faint, as if coming from a room far away. She did not force the memory. She had learned that remembering a person was not the same as recovering every detail of their life.
Above them the stars appeared one by one. They were not in the positions that old books prescribed. Mira took out a clean sheet of paper and began a map of the sky as it was, knowing that the map would need to be changed tomorrow.
!jump epilogue_open

@scene epilogue_open
!background observatory_dawn
!music dawn
!show mira open at left
A year later the children of AsterVale and Bellwether planted two trees on the observatory's western terrace. One had grown from a seed carried across the boundary, the other from the market where the blue door had stood. They had different leaves and were planted close enough that in a decade their branches would touch.
A small plaque beneath them said: WE WERE NOT SAVED BY FORGETTING. WE WERE SAVED WHEN WE LEARNED TO REMEMBER TOGETHER. Mira thought it a little too grand for the trees, which were interested in soil and sunlight and would survive without any inscription at all. She did not remove it.
The new atlas lay open in the library. Its pages were not complete. They never would be. In the empty spaces, Mira had begun writing the most difficult words a map could contain: Here, someone may live whom we have not yet met.
The End: THE OPEN SKY.

@scene ending_witness
!background undersea_lens
!music memory
!panel parchment
!show mira luminous at left
!show cael wary at right
Mira chose the second outcome because she could see no other way to protect both histories without asking anyone else to disappear. The machine required a witness who would remain inside it, continuously naming the connections between the hidden cities and the world outside. A person could become that witness. Their body would be held in the glass. Their voice would travel along the bridges, keeping the passage open as long as they could remember.
Cael: No. There has to be another way.
Mira: There may be. I don't have the right to make everyone wait for me to find it.
He turned away, then came back with an anger that frightened her because it was so carefully controlled. He had lost his sister once to a rescue he had not agreed to. He could not watch another person volunteer to vanish into the mechanism that had taken her.
Cael: You are describing yourself as though you were only a useful piece of equipment.
Mira: I am describing a choice I have made.
Cael: Then let me tell you what that choice costs the people who love you. It costs them your ordinary voice and your terrible habit of leaving ink on every surface. It costs them your questions after midnight and the way you look at the sky as though it owes you an argument. Don't call that a small price because the machine knows how to measure it.
Mira listened. She did not change her mind. She did change the words she intended to speak. If she were to enter the lens, she would do so without pretending that becoming useful made her less human.
She asked the elected delegates whether they would accept a passage kept open by a living witness. The delegates conferred. The child representing the future demanded that the witness be permitted to speak from inside the lens and that the agreement contain a provision for release if another method could be found. The adults agreed. Mira wrote the clause in language the instrument could understand and the people could revise.
!sound bell
The glass opened around her. The first sensation was of being remembered by something enormous. Every name Mira had ever read passed through her, then every name she had forgotten, then the names of people she could not have known. There were children on islands beyond the maps and people asleep in cities that had been hidden for centuries. Their histories did not merge. They became visible beside one another, like separate books placed on a shelf.
Mira stood in a room made of starlight. The eastern lens was a window in each wall. She could see AsterVale, Bellwether, the observatory, and the seas below the clouds. She could not leave the room. The new clause had placed her there by choice, but choice did not make the walls less real.
She heard Cael calling her name. At first his voice came from a distance that made the room seem impossibly large. Then she found the place in the glass where her palm met his, and the distance shortened until she could see every line on his face.
Mira: I am here.
Cael: I know. I'm going to find a way to make those words mean you can come out.
Mira: Then I will keep a record of every attempt, including the failed ones. Especially the failed ones.
For a while the arrangement worked. The hidden cities reopened their passages. Their residents traveled between the islands with passports issued by both governments. The storm remained beyond the boundary, but its pressure became a weather pattern rather than a catastrophe. Mira's presence in the instrument prevented the histories from colliding and gave the people time to build bridges that were not made only of light.
The cost was loneliness. She could visit every part of the world through the lens but could touch almost none of it. Each morning she watched the market open in AsterVale and wondered what fresh bread smelled like. Each evening she watched the observatory library darken and remembered how the polished marble felt cold beneath her feet. She had chosen the sacrifice. She was not obliged to enjoy every part of it.
Cael visited as often as the sky routes allowed. Sometimes he brought news about Elian, who had become a teacher and was organizing the records of the House of Returns into a public archive. Sometimes he brought gossip about the Chancellor's trial or the latest argument between the islands about the taxation of bridges. Sometimes he brought nothing at all and sat beside the glass while Mira spoke about the star patterns only she could see.
!if bond >= 2
One winter evening he told her he loved her. He did not put the words in the shape of a plea. Mira had spent her life becoming suspicious of promises that asked another person to change their choices. She answered that she loved him too, which was both true and infuriatingly impractical. They laughed at the cruelty of timing, and then they began planning a future neither would promise the other was guaranteed.
!end
!if bond < 2
They became friends in the difficult, durable sense: people who kept returning to one another even when the visits made them aware of what was missing. Their disagreements remained. Their loyalty did not require them to pretend the disagreements had ceased.
!end
Years passed. The observation room acquired a chair made from a tree that had grown in Bellwether before the erasure. It was a poor chair, because wood brought across a memory boundary did not hold its shape perfectly, but Mira loved the feel of it. A young engineer from AsterVale discovered how to send objects into the lens without paying a toll. The first successful object was a cup of tea. Mira cried when she tasted it, then was embarrassed and cried again.
The engineer returned with a team. They found that the lens had been designed to require a witness only because the Council's old agreement made memory a private resource. Once the people of the connected cities began sharing their histories in public archives, the machine could distribute the work of witnessing. Mira's task became smaller. One day she found she could step outside the glass for the length of a breath.
She did not leave that day. The possibility frightened her more than she had expected. She had lived in the room long enough to make its limits part of her idea of herself. Freedom, she discovered, could require a kind of courage that sacrifice did not. In the following weeks she took one step, then two, then the length of the eastern gallery. Cael stood beside the door and refused to count aloud.
!if wonder >= 5
The Keeper of the Threshold helped design the final opening. It insisted that memory ought never to depend on one person alone. The new system drew strength from the names publicly read by the schools, libraries, and families of both worlds. The Keeper called this a chorus rather than an agreement, and Mira thought the word was better.
!end
On the morning she finally walked into the observatory without a wall of glass between her and the world, the building was much changed. Vines had grown through the western windows. The upper gallery had been repaired with wood from a hidden city's trees. The table where the atlas had first opened of its own accord stood near the door, scarred and covered in the kind of disorder that means people are working.
Mira put her hand on the marble. It was cold. She laughed because the ordinary sensation made her more grateful than any of the stars she had learned to see.
!jump epilogue_witness

@scene epilogue_witness
!background observatory_dawn
!music dawn
!show mira open at left
The cities created a new office called the Department of Shared Memory. Its primary purpose was to prevent anyone from making an agreement on behalf of people who had not been asked. Its secondary purpose was to archive contradictory accounts without forcing them to agree. Mira served as its first director for two years, after which she resigned because she had been offered the chance to travel on a skyship that was not entirely reliable.
At the farewell dinner Cael returned her a watch he had repaired. It still had no hands. He said it was a reminder that time did not belong to the instruments that claimed to measure it. Mira wore it anyway.
The final page of her account contained one sentence: I was not wrong to give myself for the world; I would have been wrong to believe the world required me to remain lost.
The End: THE LIVING WITNESS.

@scene ending_covenant
!background observatory_dawn
!music memory
!panel parchment
!show mira open at left
!show cael steady at right
Mira did not choose to open the sky that morning. Nor did she accept the Chancellor's request to preserve the old agreement for another generation without alteration. She asked the delegates for a third kind of clause: a temporary covenant, recorded in public, lasting only until the next solstice, during which the cities would remain held apart but the people could cross freely, their histories could be shared, and any resident could challenge the terms in a court chosen by both worlds.
Orsol: You are using an emergency instrument to found a government.
Mira: Yes. It is better than using an emergency to excuse the absence of one.
There were objections. The machine had not been designed to distribute its power. The Council lacked the authority to surrender an agreement it had made in secret. The delegates feared that another delay would become permanent, as delays so often do when the people who benefit from them are allowed to define what counts as a solution.
Mira addressed each objection without pretending any of them was foolish. She proposed a date that could not be extended without a vote from both cities and the outer islands, a public archive of every payment the instrument had taken, and a right of passage for families separated by the boundary. She insisted on an accounting of the missing people even if the missing could not yet be returned.
Elian spoke from the eastern lens. She wanted the covenant written in a language that the children of AsterVale could understand. A boy beside her asked whether the document would allow him to visit the sea. The delegates added a clause guaranteeing travel. A woman whose father had died outside the boundary asked whether it would allow her to visit his grave. Another clause was added, though the words had to be revised many times before they stopped sounding like a promise the machine might interpret too narrowly.
!sound page
The atlas recorded each amendment. The Chancellor watched with an expression of genuine dismay as the agreement grew from a page of neat mathematical balances into a document that admitted how many people had been left outside the original calculation. The new contract was ugly by the standards of elegant law. It was full of exceptions, responsibilities, and rights that required people to speak and listen and argue. Mira trusted it more for that.
The Keeper of the Threshold placed its hand on the empty bottle marked Again. The other bottles opened one by one. From them came voices: the first directors, their assistants, the families who had been erased to pay the toll, and the people who had signed the original rescue agreement. Their voices disagreed. The Keeper did not silence them. The covenant became binding only when it had recorded the disagreement along with the decision.
A bridge of light formed between AsterVale and the eastern observatory. It was narrow enough that two people had to turn sideways to pass one another. From the hidden side came a girl carrying a blue scarf, and from the outer side came a man whose old watch had no hands. They met in the middle and embraced for a long time while the machine continued transcribing the terms.
Mira felt relief, and then suspicion. Temporary solutions had a habit of becoming respectable cages. She wrote the covenant's expiration in large letters at the top of the first page and repeated it at the bottom: this agreement will end whether or not the Council is ready to admit that it has ended.
Cael: That seems very emphatic.
Mira: I have been studying bureaucrats all my life. They are excellent at turning temporary discomfort into inherited architecture.
The storm quieted. It did not disappear. Around the boundary the air still carried competing histories, and people crossing the bridge sometimes returned with memories that did not belong to them. Mira called these conditions risks rather than prohibitions and posted clear notices at every passage. Nobody was forced to cross. Nobody was forbidden merely because crossing might make their life difficult.
The first month was chaos. People arrived with bills addressed to cities that had never appeared on the outside's maps. Families reunited, discovered they had become strangers, and began the slower task of learning one another again. Courts disagreed about property. Cartographers complained that the boundary moved too much to be drawn. Children insisted on drawing it anyway, usually with dragons in the empty places.
The second month was harder. A ship carrying medical supplies vanished in the storm and reappeared three days later with its crew alive and two passengers unaccounted for. Newspapers demanded that the bridge be closed. Orsol said the disaster proved her original policy had been necessary. Mira answered that a public government could investigate a disaster without pretending the people harmed had never existed.
!if truth >= 5
The investigation disclosed that a former Council official had tampered with the navigation charts to demonstrate the failure of the covenant. The trial was public. The accused was permitted a defense, the witnesses were allowed to tell their stories, and the court recorded the incident without deleting inconvenient evidence. Mira considered this an achievement more significant than the recovery of the ship.
!end
!if mercy >= 5
The mothers and fathers who had lost children in the erased districts formed a mutual-aid network. Its first rule was that no one should be made to tell their story twice to receive help. Its second rule was that people should be permitted to tell their story whenever they wished. The network became more effective at finding the missing than the Council's entire department of restitution.
!end
On the night before the solstice, people gathered on both sides of the bridge. The covenant was due to expire at dawn. There had been six months of argument and risk, but there had also been six months in which a child could wake and know that someone in another city remembered her name. The delegates proposed a permanent public body to oversee the passage and an engineering commission to dismantle the machine's toll mechanism without closing the boundary.
Mira looked at the old atlas. Its blank places had begun filling with notes written in many different hands. It was no longer one scholar's instrument; it had become a record of the questions people asked when they refused to accept a single answer.
The vote took place in the market and in the outer islands at the same hour. The majority approved the new agreement, but a substantial minority opposed it. The minority's objections were recorded, and the new body was given a legal duty to revisit them. It was an unsatisfying conclusion for people who wanted history to end with a victory parade. Mira thought it a promising beginning for people who intended to live in history after the parade went home.
Orsol resigned before the vote. She gave Mira a sealed envelope containing the names of the people she had personally ordered erased. The list was incomplete. She did not ask for forgiveness when she handed it over. Mira could not tell whether that was dignity or shame. The Commission would determine what responsibility required; Mira no longer believed the Chancellor's feelings were the center of the problem.
!if bond >= 2
Cael returned from Bellwether with a repaired telescope and two tickets for the first public skyship to AsterVale. He had included an open invitation to Elian, who was now old enough to decline it. Mira accepted the journey, though she insisted they bring the notebooks. Their travels would be work, she said. Cael agreed in the tone of a man who intended to take her argument seriously and then find ways to make the work feel like an adventure.
!end
!if bond < 2
Cael took charge of the new courier route between the cities. He sent Mira letters from each island, half practical reports and half stories about people he had met. The letters were badly organized, but they arrived. Mira kept them in a box labeled EVIDENCE OF AN INCONVENIENTLY RELIABLE FRIEND.
!end
At the second solstice, no one remembered whose idea it had been to write the covenant on temporary paper. That was good. The agreement no longer belonged to its authors. It belonged to the people who revised it, taught it, argued about it, and lived under it. Mira had learned enough about the cost of being remembered to find a certain comfort in becoming less important to the story.
!jump epilogue_covenant

@scene epilogue_covenant
!background hidden_market
!music dawn
The first new bridge was built of ordinary stone. It took eight years, cost more money than anyone had expected, and required engineers from both worlds to agree on a definition of level ground. When it was finished, the mayor of AsterVale and the governor of the outer islands crossed it from opposite ends. They met in the middle, shook hands, and immediately began disagreeing about the toll for freight wagons.
Mira watched from a bench with a book in her lap. She had written a history of the covenant but left the last chapter blank. The bridge was proof that people could finish a structure without finishing an argument. She wanted the book to leave room for that distinction.
A little boy ran past holding two maps, one drawn by his mother and one by his grandfather. Their coastlines did not match. He asked which was the true one. Mira told him to look at the sea and make a third.
The End: THE SOLSTICE COVENANT.

@scene ending_unbound
!background shattered_lens
!music storm
!panel parchment
!show mira troubled at left
!show cael resolved at right
Mira laid the atlas beneath the empty bottle and told the instrument its own history. It had been built to make the world legible, modified to make people invisible, and used to extract payments from people who had never been allowed to read the contract. The Council had called the arrangement a necessary evil. Mira had spent the night learning that necessities became especially dangerous when their names were used to end a conversation.
She chose to destroy the machine rather than renew the bargain. The delegates had authorized the end of compulsory erasure, and she would not sign another agreement that required names to be taken without consent. But she also refused the instrument's offer to reunite every street by force. If the machine ceased to exist, the worlds would remain partly separate until the people who lived in them found their own ways across.
Cael: That may take centuries.
Mira: Then we will have to be patient with the centuries.
He looked at his scar. The compass rose had begun to glow, its lines connecting to the great ring above the lens. He could open a passage by using the mark as a key, but once the machine was gone the key might burn out with it. He did not ask Mira to change her choice. He asked her whether she was prepared to live with a world in which some doors remained closed for reasons she could not fix.
Mira: I am frightened of it. That is not a reason to give the doors to the Chancellor again.
!sound bell
She turned the empty bottle in the opposite direction from the old agreement. It broke. Across the lens, all the names that had been held as payment appeared at once. They did not return to their original places. They became a field of light that covered the clouds from horizon to horizon. For one terrible moment Mira could see every person who had ever been erased and every person who had lost someone to the instrument. The living and the dead, the remembered and the forgotten, were together in a picture the world had never been able to bear.
Then the picture dissolved. The great rings stopped turning. The observatory shook as the stored memories rushed out through the eastern tower, and the glass of the lens fractured into a thousand pieces. Each fragment contained a place: an orchard, a river, a stairwell, a kitchen where salt was scattered across a window ledge. They fell like rain, but did not cut anyone who touched them. Mira caught one in her hand and saw her father standing before a mirror. He was younger than she remembered, afraid, and trying to write an apology he knew would not be enough.
The fragment broke against her skin. She remembered his voice.
It was not an enormous revelation. He had said her name in the kitchen on an ordinary morning when she was six, asking her to put on her shoes because they were late. Mira sat down on the floor and cried because the memory was so small, so human, and so entirely beyond the reach of a machine built to calculate what a person ought to be allowed to lose.
Cael sat beside her. He did not tell her that the sacrifice was worth it. He did not tell her the pain would fade. They remained together while the observatory came apart in slow, echoing noises, and Mira was grateful he had learned not to improve a difficult moment by putting an argument inside it.
The hidden cities did not immediately return. Their streets remained beyond the old boundary, visible in the sky like islands through fog. But the machine no longer demanded payment for keeping them there. The erasures stopped. People who had been losing memories woke to find that they could remember yesterday without the fear that tomorrow would take it away.
There were no new bridges of light. The governments were furious. The Chancellor's advisors called the decision reckless, an abandonment of the only technology capable of saving the cities. Some residents of AsterVale agreed. They had lived all their lives with the hope that someone would repair the machine and bring them home. Mira did not dismiss their anger. She had made a choice whose consequences would outlast her, and she was obliged to hear from people who believed it had been the wrong one.
Orsol: You have destroyed the means of reconciliation.
Mira: I have destroyed the means of coercion. If reconciliation was possible only through that instrument, we will have to learn to make it possible without one.
Orsol walked away without answering. Mira never knew whether the Chancellor began to understand or simply found another audience that would listen to her certainty. The public inquiry continued. Some of the records were incomplete now that the machine was gone; the people who held them became witnesses. That was difficult, sometimes contradictory, often painful. It was also the first honest account of the catastrophe anyone had attempted.
Cael's scar faded over several weeks. He could no longer open the glass passage by placing his hand against it. His first thought was of Elian. He had found her, and now she was on the other side again. But Elian had kept the papers on which she had drawn her windows. She sent them through the old post route, which had begun to work in strange ways after the instrument's destruction. Some letters arrived with the corners burned blue, some arrived a week before they were sent, and a few never arrived at all. Cael took over the route because he believed people deserved a courier who would keep trying even when the roads refused to acknowledge his map.
Mira joined him after the first winter. She had spent months studying the fragments of the broken lens. They carried no power to erase or preserve a city, but they could reveal the places where two histories overlapped. With the fragments as guides, ordinary navigators began building slow passages across the boundary, small enough for one person at a time. The first successful crossing was made by a woman who wanted to attend her father's funeral. The second carried a shipment of medicine. The third brought a child home to a mother who had kept an extra place at the table for years.
!if mercy >= 4
The woman in the red coat found her daughter after five months of searching. There was no miracle, only a patchwork of records, drawings, remembered stories, and people willing to keep asking questions in the right places. Mira was there when the daughter came home. The mother had made too much bread and kept apologizing for the mess. The daughter laughed and said she would like the recipe, which neither woman could remember. They invented a new one together.
!end
!if wonder >= 4
The Keeper of the Threshold survived the instrument's destruction. It had become a child who could remember the names of people it had never met, and it found this ability both useful and exhausting. It attended school in AsterVale, where it was delighted to learn that forgetting one's homework was a common event and not a sign of a cosmic disaster.
!end
Years later Mira returned to the observatory, where the eastern tower was now open to the wind. Someone had planted vines in the empty ring that once held the lens. The vines followed the old circular paths and bloomed in spring with small blue flowers. Visitors came to see the ruins and ask questions about the ancient machine. Some wanted it rebuilt. Others wanted the last fragments destroyed. Mira kept the remaining pieces under glass, with the names of the people who had once lived behind their light.
She was older, and the maps she carried had become more complicated. They included annotations where the roads were uncertain, estimates of the dangers at the boundary, names of people who could help a traveler, and blank spaces where knowledge was not yet available. They were less elegant than the old atlas. They were more useful.
Cael came to meet her on the terrace. Elian had sent a letter. She was planning to visit in the summer, traveling through the slow crossing near the northern lighthouse. She wanted to bring her students and show them the observatory, which they had seen only in history books. The letter smelled faintly of sugared pears.
Mira: Do you ever wish I had chosen another ending?
Cael: Often. I also wish my sister had never been taken away, and that the old skyship company had paid me more. Wishing is not a final judgment.
Mira: What is?
Cael: Ask me after we have lived a little longer.
They looked at the open sky. It was full of roads that had not yet been built. Mira unfolded a new map and began marking the place where the stars had once been divided by a lens. She did not know whether the world would ever be whole again. She knew that the work of making it more connected could not be outsourced to a machine that demanded people be forgotten.
!jump epilogue_unbound

@scene epilogue_unbound
!background observatory_evening
!music dawn
!show mira open at left
In the old observatory library a child found the Atlas of Unwritten Skies. Its pages had become ordinary paper. The coastal outlines were incomplete, the ink faded in places, and the book no longer opened itself when someone spoke a name. She carried it to the terrace and asked Mira whether it was broken.
Mira turned a page. There was a blank region where the hidden cities had once been, but beside it someone had written a note in blue pencil: Here are people we have not yet reached. We are looking for a way.
Mira: No. I think it's finally doing the job a map was meant to do.
The child asked whether she might draw a bridge in the empty place. Mira gave her the pencil. The bridge was crooked and led nowhere in particular. It was the first bridge the child had ever drawn. They kept it in the atlas.
The End: THE UNBOUND WORLD.
